WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:03.510
(upbeat electronic music)

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.460
- Welcome to Ear Biscuits.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.150
I'm Link.
- And I'm Rhett.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.220
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.840
we're going to be exploring the question

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.330
did our careers just peak?

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:17.510
- We are fresh off of

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:20.880
a tour, a little mini
tour, we went to St. Louis,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.483
Columbus, Washington D.C. and Nashville.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.340
And in Nashville, the
venue was very special

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.050
because it was the mother
church of country music,

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.853
the Ryman Auditorium.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.440
Yeah so I mean you just mentioned to me

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.453
and we translated it into
the question of this episode.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.360
You said that the thought
that crossed your mind,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.393
is this the peak of our career?

00:00:52.512 --> 00:00:55.340
- Yeah.
- Yeah I think,

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.803
so we wanna talk to you
about the whole tour.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:00.810
- And I was saying--
- But I'm certain we will

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.380
reflect on that specifically.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.820
- P-E-E-K peek.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.650
- Not did our careers just lean over

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.240
and look around a corner at us.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.470
- No yes 'cause that would be P-E-E-K.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:13.110
- Oh that is what you meant.
- Yeah.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.610
Oh you were talking about P-E-A-K.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.850
- Yes.
- Or you talking about

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:18.810
P-I-Q-U-E pique?

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:19.643
- Not that.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.513
What do you mean by P-E-E-K?

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.460
You're just, you're being humorous.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.100
- I'm just doing some spelling humor.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.370
- Okay.
- It's one of my favorite

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:33.360
things to do on a podcast
'cause spelling humor

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.860
goes really well in the audio.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:40.090
I mean I always look for
a chance to spell pique

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:41.890
P-I-Q-U-E.
- Three different ways

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.450
including that way, okay.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:47.980
- If your interest is piqued,
just a little grammar lesson.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.720
It's P-I-Q-U-E-D.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.720
It's not P-E-A-K-E-D like it
was the peak of your interest.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.280
- And if I peek around a corner

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.400
and that piques your interest,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.240
then that may be a peak moment for you.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.080
- Right.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:04.750
- Right.
- Yeah and you know what,

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:05.913
spelling's important.

00:02:07.563 --> 00:02:10.690
- So we kinda wanna go through,

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.290
spelling's important.
- It is!

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:13.339
- Put that on a t-shirt.

00:02:13.339 --> 00:02:16.710
- Listen, I watch, do
you watch your kids--

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.040
- Spell, no, it sounds quite boring.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.978
- No like, have you ever
gone up behind your kid--

00:02:21.978 --> 00:02:23.400
- Hey kid, you mind if I
look over your shoulder

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.233
and watch you spell?

00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:26.180
- While they write a paper.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.520
- I've read their papers, yeah.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.510
- You've never been
with them when they were

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:30.770
in the process of writing a paper?

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.170
Now listen, I can relate to this

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:37.592
because I've become incredibly
dependent on spell-check.

00:02:37.592 --> 00:02:38.840
- Yeah.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.423
- But kids these days, man.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:42.611
- They got it bad.

00:02:42.611 --> 00:02:47.611
- They stumble over like the
and it's autocorrected for 'em.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.640
- How else would you spell the?

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.140
- T-H-E-E.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.250
- That's also correct.
- T-H-E-A.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.280
T-H-I-Q-U-E.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.730
- Your kids don't know how to spell the?

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:00.173
- That's thique.
- Man.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:03.830
So we're gonna spell
through a bunch of that,

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:08.140
but one of the things,
you don't have a lot

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:11.088
of time in each town, we
did four, those four dates

00:03:11.088 --> 00:03:13.380
over the course of five days.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.430
So it's pretty aggressive but you do find

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:20.430
these little pockets of time
to experience a little sliver

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.169
of the locale.

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:26.410
And one of the things that we've noticed

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.930
that was that was across
all of these cities,

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:30.830
I think it's something
that's sweeping the nation.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.805
- Sweeping.
- Maybe the world.

00:03:32.805 --> 00:03:34.610
Of course, I didn't see them in London

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:36.953
or did I?

00:03:36.953 --> 00:03:38.560
No I don't think I did.
- I don't think you did

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.860
because there's a bigger
bike culture in London.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:45.860
- I'm talking about app accessed scooters.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:50.230
- I mean we are in the
peak, I believe (chuckles)

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.880
of scooters scootering
around American cities

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.340
and there's quite a
competition going right now.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.860
I mean in one, in a couple of the cities,

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.890
we saw four or five different companies.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:04.830
You got Lime, you got Bird,

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:06.690
you got Lyft gettin' into it,

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.550
you got Uber getting into
it and there was like

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.330
two other ones I can't remember the names.

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:13.960
- Like Jump, Flip, Skip, Hop.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:14.840
- Something like that.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.480
- Roller Buddies.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.930
- Roller Buddies doesn't fit on a scooter.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:23.622
- Two Wheels One Cup I
think was one of them.

00:04:23.622 --> 00:04:25.950
- I think it's Two Wheels One Foot.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.270
- That's it, that was it, that's ours.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.269
- Two Wheels No Helmet,
that's what they call me.

00:04:30.269 --> 00:04:31.600
(both chuckle)

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.820
Because incidentally that's another thing,

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:37.430
anytime, now first of all
you got to download the apps

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.240
for each one of these services

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.000
and then like the first
time you download the app,

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.540
you sign...

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.710
- You agree to the terms--
- Agree to a lot of things

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:51.490
and one of the terms is
course you will wear a helmet,

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:56.490
but literally no one on
any scooter at any time

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.170
that we saw had a helmet on.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.930
- Well I mean so the when
we were in St. Louis,

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:07.710
I mean we we we went to a
barbecue place and then we went to

00:05:08.662 --> 00:05:13.662
the Gateway Arch, which I
believe is 630 feet tall.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.280
- Well that's--
- I looked that up

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:18.840
and then I don't remember
numbers so I'm probably wrong,

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.460
but I think I'm right.
- It feels right.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.180
- I looked it up very recently.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:25.133
Well we were gonna go up
there but we didn't have time

00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:26.920
to book a tour.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:31.920
You can take a frickin'
sideways or arch path elevator

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:34.760
to the top of this thing
but we couldn't do that

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:36.880
so we just walked around the bottom of it

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.970
and we're walking around down there

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:44.970
and you got all these pavers
and it's undulating terrain,

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:47.970
just little hills but there are some steps

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:52.350
and there are some steps that
then become undulating terrain

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:57.350
and ramps and as we're walking, it was me,

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.910
Rhett and Britton, and
I see these two girls,

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:03.610
they were probably like 15.

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:07.810
And they were careening in front of us

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:11.370
on two of these scooters
and the girl in front

00:06:12.484 --> 00:06:16.393
zips by us and then she
turns around and she yells,

00:06:17.247 --> 00:06:20.310
"Don't go on the stairs!"

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:25.100
And that drew my eye to her
so I didn't actually see

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:27.230
what happened but you did.

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:30.286
- Well the way that the terrain was set up

00:06:30.286 --> 00:06:32.800
is there was a blind spot essentially

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.390
so from your perspective it just looked

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:38.220
you were gonna continue on straight ground

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.130
but really there was like a three,

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:42.083
maybe two or three step drop.

00:06:43.584 --> 00:06:46.720
- Of concrete.
- Yeah and the steps were

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:49.530
such that the further left you were,

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.170
the more steps there were to go down

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:53.590
and she just happened to
choose a line that was two

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:56.270
or three steps, like two
or three feet to the left

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.320
and it would have been five or six steps.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:01.780
Anyway.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:03.340
- Did she take flight?

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:06.593
- Well like you said, her
friend said at the last minute,

00:07:07.767 --> 00:07:09.660
"Don't go off there," or whatever,

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.640
and that was exactly
the wrong thing to say

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:15.670
because she basically
dismounted in the air

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.950
and the scooter landed
really, really hard.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.060
She actually landed on her feet.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.330
It could have been really, really ugly.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:24.150
- And she landed on her feet
and was like immediately like

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:26.015
plop plop plop plop plop like running

00:07:26.015 --> 00:07:28.513
in a very awkward fashion.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:33.770
She could have hit her head
on the concrete and died, man.

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:34.990
- We could have witnessed a death.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:37.180
- Well I could have witnessed
her friend witnessing a death

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:38.630
'cause I was looking at the wrong girl.

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:40.570
- Well and that's the main
thing that I'm thinking

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:43.080
the whole time I'm on there,
they go like 20 miles per hour

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.970
and faster than that if
you're going downhill

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:49.154
and the wheels are relatively small.

00:07:49.154 --> 00:07:53.580
There's not necessarily,
you're supposed to be riding

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:57.310
in bike lanes when possible
but most American cities

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.150
are not very bike-friendly so--

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:00.313
- Right.
- There's very few bike lanes.

00:08:00.313 --> 00:08:01.261
- [Link] A lot of people

00:08:01.261 --> 00:08:02.094
on sidewalks.
- People don't know where

00:08:02.094 --> 00:08:03.730
to go, they go on the sidewalks.

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:06.080
When they're in the street,
there might be potholes.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:07.380
People don't have good balance,

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.760
people aren't wearing helmets

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:10.860
and so there's this crazy
thing that's happening

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:12.540
where all these companies are competing

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.570
against each other to
try to win this market.

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:18.020
But at the same time,
people are getting hurt.

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:22.740
- Well when we were in Austin,
there was this makeup woman

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:23.960
and we happened to start talking to her

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:26.860
about the scooters 'cause
we saw 'em around Austin

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:29.890
and she said, yeah, my husband was on one

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:34.890
and he went off a curb and
fell over and hurt his leg,

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:39.560
and really badly, he was in a lot of pain

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.900
and the paramedics show
up and after awhile,

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:44.870
they were like you're okay, just go home.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:48.400
And he went home that night and he woke up

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.740
in excruciating pain
and she said that he was

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:54.500
in so much pain that he
started to go into shock,

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.490
and so she took him to the emergency room

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:59.540
and he had broken his femur.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:01.590
On a freakin' scooter, guys.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:02.950
- Yeah.
- And the helmet doesn't

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:04.600
protect the femur last time I checked.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:05.870
- Well if you get a femur helmet.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:10.870
- That's what we need so Two
Wheels One Foot is gonna,

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:15.577
we're gonna figure out a
way to attach the helmet

00:09:15.577 --> 00:09:18.720
to the scooter and also
have like femur pads.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:19.849
- Right.
- I mean you can really

00:09:19.849 --> 00:09:24.060
get hurt because you think
you can ride a scooter

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:26.470
but you don't realize how
fast these things can go

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:30.900
and also you're tempted to
just steer like left, right,

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.480
left, right, inky inky inky

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:34.550
but you really should steer by leaning.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:35.850
- You need to be using your body weight.

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:37.460
- You gotta be using your body weight

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:41.125
but that's not immediately intuitive

00:09:41.125 --> 00:09:43.806
and if you start doing
that then you're doing

00:09:43.806 --> 00:09:46.610
the shaky shaky shaky thing
and then you're like going,

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:48.360
you're careening out of control.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.515
- And people are, locals hate
these things, I mean so--

00:09:51.515 --> 00:09:53.765
- They have to.
- When we were in Nashville,

00:09:55.696 --> 00:10:00.230
by that point, Britton's
friends had showed up.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:03.120
Jenna was with us at
the time so it was like,

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:08.080
there was six of us in a
little pod going down--

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.710
- The street.
- Basically the main drag

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:14.010
in Nashville and as we
continued going down,

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:17.403
we started realizing this
has almost become a highway.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.640
People are turning off of
this to get on the interstate.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:23.660
There's only a few stop lights.

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:25.150
There's no bike lane.

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:27.860
We're right up, we're
going across a bridge

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.590
and the six of us in a line
are kinda taking up the lane

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:34.560
and people are just laying on
their horns and locals hate us

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.330
and they should.
(Link mimics horn honking)

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:37.530
- But we were doing the right thing

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.150
because you're not supposed
to be on the sidewalk,

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:41.630
you're supposed to be like a bike.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:42.940
But they were just laying on the horn,

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:45.543
I guess it was because
some of us were waggling.

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:48.270
- It goes beyond that
though because I think what

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:50.870
they're trying to do is they're
trying to say don't be here.

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:52.760
Go on a less busy street.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:54.413
Wear a freaking helmet.

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:58.370
And we were talking to--
- Go back to whatever town

00:10:58.370 --> 00:10:59.940
you're from--
- Talking to Britton's friends

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:03.844
and apparently in Raleigh, North Carolina,

00:11:03.844 --> 00:11:06.720
they've gotten rid of all of 'em.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:07.553
- Oh really?

00:11:07.553 --> 00:11:08.760
- They've just gotten
rid of all the scooters

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.200
is what we were told, I haven't
been there to confirm this.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.550
Because they couldn't figure
out how to regulate them

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:16.954
and then the companies were like screw it,

00:11:16.954 --> 00:11:18.540
we're not gonna be a part of this.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.320
It just don't know
where this is gonna land

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:23.840
because people are getting hurt

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.370
and they're gonna continue to get hurt.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:27.040
Personally, I love it.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:28.430
- When we were--
- I absolutely love it.

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:30.423
- Yeah so when we were in Columbus,

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:35.160
I looked out my hotel window
and I saw a couple people.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:39.270
We were downtown by the river
and there's nice river walks

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.500
on either side of it and I
saw some people with scooters,

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:43.630
I was like (snaps fingers) bingo.

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:46.260
We're gonna do it 'cause we
didn't do it in St. Louis.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:50.369
I just saw that girl
almost eat it and then,

00:11:50.369 --> 00:11:52.920
the next morning, here I am
still thinking it's a great idea

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:54.482
and you know what, it was.

00:11:54.482 --> 00:11:55.870
- Oh it's so much fun.
- It was fun.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:59.720
I mean when you're not just
going on the surface streets

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:02.540
like a downtown area
with a lot of traffic,

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:07.540
but you're on a dedicated path
and a relatively fresh one

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:11.490
that doesn't have a
bunch of treachery to it,

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.620
that was fun, man, the weather was great.

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:15.030
- Yeah.
- It was beautiful.

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:19.470
It was a highlight of the
trip was scooting around, man.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:20.303
Yay!

00:12:21.609 --> 00:12:22.442
Woo, woo!

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:28.440
- Being off of the road,
I would say that being

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:33.440
on a not busy road or
being on like a park path,

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:38.540
park path, that is peak scooter terrain.

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:41.050
- But you know what, I think
I'm gonna start traveling

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:42.413
with a helmet.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:44.800
Why are you laughing?

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:46.373
I got to get a little helmet, man.

00:12:46.373 --> 00:12:48.940
You laugh now.

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:51.280
But you're not gonna laugh
when you don't remember how

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:53.900
to laugh 'cause you hit your head.

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:55.400
- There's something about
being on a scooter though

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.680
that makes me feel, because
my feet are so close

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:02.130
to the ground already, I
feel like I can just jump

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:05.810
out of any situation, any
danger and just immediately jump

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:08.050
and be standing up whereas a motorcycle,

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:10.400
you're going a lot faster--
- Yeah.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:13.070
Your feet are real close to
the ground on a motorcycle too.

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:15.380
- But you can't just
walk off of a motorcycle.

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:17.100
- If you ride in a side saddle you can.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:19.435
- Yeah I don't ride my
motorcycle side saddle anymore.

00:13:19.435 --> 00:13:22.020
- How do you ride your horse, side saddle?

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:23.520
- Backwards.

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:25.610
I don't want anybody sneakin' up on me.

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:27.645
- I ride my horses backwards.

00:13:27.645 --> 00:13:29.178
(Rhett chuckles)

00:13:29.178 --> 00:13:31.130
(Link chuckles)

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:35.760
So I don't know what the future
of scootering is gonna be

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:39.090
but they can't tether
the helmets to the thing

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:41.590
because then that's a hazard.

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:43.360
- Lice.
- Oh lice too.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:45.423
- Yeah that's a lice hazard.
Is that what you mean by that?

00:13:45.423 --> 00:13:48.143
- No I mean if there's a string, a tether,

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.050
so that people won't walk
away with the helmets.

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:54.840
I think you need a helmet sharing app.

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:56.220
- Same company, different company?

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:57.160
- Different company.

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:58.520
- We should do the helmet share.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:01.150
- So you're gonna have
to walk to the helmet

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:04.040
and then the problem
people would be scootering

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:04.873
to the helmets.

00:14:04.873 --> 00:14:08.770
- No you gotta sign in, no no
it's gotta be the same company

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:10.790
and you can't, it's like
one of those things,

00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:13.340
after you get a few DUIs
you can't start a car

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:17.160
without breathing into
it, you have to step on.

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:20.059
You have to hit--
- You gotta bonk your head.

00:14:20.059 --> 00:14:22.358
- You gotta head butt the
scooter with the helmet

00:14:22.358 --> 00:14:23.280
in order to get it going.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:25.194
- Or just be willing to head
butt the scooter really hard

00:14:25.194 --> 00:14:27.950
without a helmet to make it seem like--

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:31.138
- I think if they had a helmet
that was like a zip tie,

00:14:31.138 --> 00:14:35.040
not zip tie but like a zip
line, like it comes out

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.740
from the handlebars and
then you're kinda like

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:41.010
loosely tethered to the thing.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:42.160
- It's kinda like--
- That sounds like it might

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:44.232
be more dangerous.
- Kinda like it's got

00:14:44.232 --> 00:14:46.903
a narrow roof on it.

00:14:48.144 --> 00:14:50.190
It's like, have you ever seen those bikes

00:14:50.190 --> 00:14:52.210
that would have like a shell on 'em?

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:54.060
- Yeah.
- Like an aerodynamic shell,

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:56.040
have one of those come
off the handlebars, joop,

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:57.400
goes over your whole head.

00:14:57.400 --> 00:14:58.722
- Then it's just a little car.

00:14:58.722 --> 00:15:01.130
(Link chuckles)

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:02.730
That's not fun, you don't
feel the breeze anymore.

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:04.470
I wanna feel the breeze,
that's half of it.

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:05.390
- Okay.
- Anyway,

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:06.570
y'all gotta figure that out.

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:08.510
And I don't know who's
gonna win this but--

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:09.343
- Yeah.
- I don't know if

00:15:09.343 --> 00:15:11.410
I would invest in,

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:16.410
my gut is that this is not sustainable.

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:19.775
But if it ends up being sustainable--

00:15:19.775 --> 00:15:20.810
- I think it is.

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:25.810
- My gut says that now that
Lyft and apparently Uber

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:32.804
is in this game, I feel like
they're gonna win because--

00:15:32.804 --> 00:15:34.050
- You already got that app.

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:37.520
- Yeah the main barrier is like
I gotta download something,

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:40.190
man, I don't have good service here.

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:41.780
- But you get so excited--
- Now I gotta take pictures

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:43.970
of my license, I don't
travel with my license.

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:46.240
- I've never been so
willing to download an app

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:49.890
as when I was about to get on
a scooter, I will say that.

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:52.410
Like when I'm about to get on a scooter,

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:54.000
I'll download any app.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.670
I mean it could be like a
credit card number sharing app,

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:03.057
like HackMe.com app,
I would download that.

00:16:03.057 --> 00:16:04.470
That's how excited I was,

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:06.250
dead set on gettin' on that scooter.

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:07.480
- That's an interesting app.

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:09.040
- I would agree to anything.

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:10.590
I mean I basically signed my life away.

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:13.186
Yeah, I'm wearing a
helmet, wink, wink, wink.

00:16:13.186 --> 00:16:15.090
You know?
- Yeah I'm hard headed.

00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:16.710
(Link chuckles)

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.170
- And then people are taking
them home and chargin' them up

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:22.090
and makin' a little
extra scratch, you know?

00:16:22.090 --> 00:16:24.300
- Yeah.
- It's good for everybody.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:26.500
And they're not gas powered.

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:27.530
Technically it's good for the--

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:29.460
- Maybe it's not too late.
- The environment.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:30.630
- Maybe we could get in on this.

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:32.280
Mythical Scooters, you know what,

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:35.233
we make ours three-wheeled.

00:16:36.069 --> 00:16:37.620
(clicks tongue) Safer.

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:38.560
- Safer.

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:39.890
- It's shaped like a pyramid.

00:16:39.890 --> 00:16:41.850
It's double-wheeled in the back.

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:44.620
- No two wheels in the front
like when a kid's learning

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:45.720
how to ride a scooter.

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:48.560
Yeah.
- Like a spider?

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:50.360
- Yeah and we market it to children.

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:53.980
That's what we do.

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:56.400
We're gonna do that but first,

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:59.980
we're just gonna promote what
we got which is this hat.

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:01.540
It's a GMM hat.

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:04.300
It's got some camouflage to
it, it's got some orange to it,

00:17:04.300 --> 00:17:05.663
it's got accoutrements.

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:09.690
- We also got mugs, the Ear Biscuits mug.

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:11.330
It's beige.

00:17:11.330 --> 00:17:12.870
- It's so good to drink out of.

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:13.703
It's hefty.

00:17:13.703 --> 00:17:16.440
Once you get this thing
you'll be like man,

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:18.400
this is a high quality mug.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:21.409
- Oh, Feldman wants me
to say that it's cream.

00:17:21.409 --> 00:17:23.750
I go with beige.

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:26.883
I think beige is one of the
most pleasing colors of nature.

00:17:28.570 --> 00:17:31.530
It is the color of most things.

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:32.620
When the world ends,

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:35.980
when the universe finally just
dissipates into nothingness,

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:38.540
it will be dissipating into beige.

00:17:38.540 --> 00:17:40.600
- Beige is the entropy of color.

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:44.110
- Beige is before all
things and after all things.

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:44.963
Beige.

00:17:47.236 --> 00:17:49.430
- I would venture to guess
before and after all the things

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:51.340
would be just blackness.

00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:52.800
- No, it's beigeness.

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:54.580
That's a common misconception.

00:17:54.580 --> 00:17:55.760
- Well I'm glad you set it straight

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:57.513
because you had that knowledge.

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:00.470
- Beigeness, in the beginning--

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:02.130
- You know where to go--
- Was beigeness.

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.810
- Mythical.store.

00:18:04.810 --> 00:18:09.660
Rep your boys, drink, drink
from the cup of biscuit.

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:16.800
- Okay so a couple of interesting
things about this tour.

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:20.720
Now as we have, as
we've stated repeatedly,

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:23.610
but it cannot be overstated apparently

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:27.070
because even some of
the VIPs who showed up

00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:32.070
to meet and greet us
after a few of the shows,

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:35.947
multiple people would say
something along these lines,

00:18:35.947 --> 00:18:39.287
"Man, I didn't know you guys played music.

00:18:39.287 --> 00:18:41.197
"I mean I thought this
was gonna be two hours

00:18:41.197 --> 00:18:42.300
"of Will It Burrito."

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:44.120
- Somebody said that specifically, yeah.

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:46.870
- Uh and listen--
- That would be a big burrito.

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:48.560
- Now I do under--
- We could do it

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:50.520
and we would do it and we might do it.

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:52.260
- Yeah you think that's a
great idea but that's like--

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.310
- We didn't just do it.
- Not this year.

00:18:55.778 --> 00:18:57.390
I think that it's a little,

00:18:57.390 --> 00:19:00.150
we have a little bit of a
branding problem, right,

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:02.991
because the thing that
we're known for the most

00:19:02.991 --> 00:19:06.410
is Good Mythical Morning
and let's be honest,

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:10.130
if you were to watch just
a little highlight reel

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:12.750
of what we do on the
show, you might not come

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:17.098
to the conclusion that these
idiots had any other talents

00:19:17.098 --> 00:19:20.020
other than not being able to eat things.

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:22.387
- Yeah I think someone said,

00:19:22.387 --> 00:19:24.950
"I didn't know you guys
were talented." (chuckles)

00:19:24.950 --> 00:19:28.550
We got, and these are like
fans paying to meet us.

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:30.520
I mean these are like
true Mythical Beasts.

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:32.660
I didn't know, but
things come out sideways

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:34.580
when you're meeting
somebody that you've watched

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:36.920
a lot on the internet so
I just find humor in it

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:38.493
but I know they didn't mean
anything by it and I'm like--

00:19:38.493 --> 00:19:41.980
- Well and on top of that,
another thing I'll say is that,

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:43.860
let's say that you're a fan of GMM

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:46.600
and you've been watching that
and then what you have seen

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:51.600
of our other work is say, our
really popular rap battles

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:54.870
or other music videos
that we made on YouTube.

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:57.430
Again, anybody can make a music video.

00:19:57.430 --> 00:19:59.410
Anybody can sing along with a track.

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:02.010
Anybody can have their voice Auto-Tuned

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:04.330
so I think a lot of people are just like,

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:06.090
oh well of course,
you've done music videos

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:09.880
but to sit down for
almost two hours for us

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:14.880
to do live instrument-based
music is something

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:18.660
that a lot of people just
aren't expecting, but again,

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:22.230
as we explained at every show,

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:24.220
one of the things that's
really cool about it

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:25.980
is that it's sort of a
returning to the roots

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:30.122
of what we did before,
before YouTube started,

00:20:30.122 --> 00:20:33.357
our comedy was primarily just funny songs

00:20:33.357 --> 00:20:36.540
that we performed live for
people and then we turned some

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:38.090
of those into music
videos and that was sort

00:20:38.090 --> 00:20:39.950
of the beginning of our comedy career.

00:20:39.950 --> 00:20:44.160
So this is kinda going
back to what started it all

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.540
and anyway--

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:48.381
- It's really fun to do.

00:20:48.381 --> 00:20:52.400
I'm very glad that this
is what we decided to do

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:55.780
when we now tour and by
the way we are continuing

00:20:55.780 --> 00:20:58.823
to tour so if you wanna come
see us, RhettAndLinkLive.com.

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:01.390
Can I plug the dates, I should--

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:02.940
- Plug the dates man, plug it.

00:21:02.940 --> 00:21:07.110
- [Link] Las Vegas on June
21st, Salt Lake City 22nd,

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:11.760
Denver the 23rd, Milwaukee the
25th, Indianapolis the 26th,

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:16.760
Detroit the 27th, Omaha the
29th, Minneapolis, the 30th.

00:21:17.615 --> 00:21:19.823
RhettAndLinkLive.com, get your tickets.

00:21:20.858 --> 00:21:24.510
I just had such a good
time because I really enjoy

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:28.100
singing the songs that
we've written over the years

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:33.131
but it also does capture a lot and I think

00:21:33.131 --> 00:21:36.820
of what people like about
GMM and that's the fact

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:39.840
that we can kinda, it's not scripted.

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:41.430
We're just kind of in between songs,

00:21:41.430 --> 00:21:43.690
we're just kinda cuttin'
up and seeing what happens.

00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:47.004
- Right.
- And on any given night,

00:21:47.004 --> 00:21:48.947
you never know what's gonna happen.

00:21:48.947 --> 00:21:51.730
At the beginning, the songs were,

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:53.960
we were a little more
rusty, by the time we got

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:55.807
to Nashville, we kinda had the songs down

00:21:55.807 --> 00:21:58.460
and we weren't gonna forget
lyrics and stuff like that,

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:01.230
but on the first night,
one of the new songs

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:04.160
when you moved over to the piano,

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:05.340
like you started playing it then

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:07.410
you never started singing it and I'm like,

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:08.750
he doesn't remember the lyrics and all

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:11.031
of a sudden you're like, I
can't remember the words,

00:22:11.031 --> 00:22:14.647
and that was the most fun part
of the night because like--

00:22:14.647 --> 00:22:16.950
- Oh for you. (chuckles)

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:17.920
Real fun for you.

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:20.040
- When something goes
wrong, it's like okay,

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:23.253
everybody in the whole place
knows this was not planned

00:22:23.253 --> 00:22:25.010
so now what are we gonna do?

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:29.209
And so that's what's fun is
when you don't have to wig out,

00:22:29.209 --> 00:22:32.551
you can just say, you can
just have fun with it,

00:22:32.551 --> 00:22:33.990
and that was fun.

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:36.660
I had fun messing with
you and I knew the lyrics

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:40.660
so I chose not to tell you and
kinda make you squirm but--

00:22:40.660 --> 00:22:41.750
- I sat there and played the piano

00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:44.011
for what felt like five minutes.

00:22:44.011 --> 00:22:46.180
- (laughs) It was funny, man.

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:48.150
- Played the intro.
- People loved it.

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:49.587
- Oh I know people loved
it I'm just saying that--

00:22:49.587 --> 00:22:52.520
- 'Cause it's honest.
- I was like I really

00:22:52.520 --> 00:22:54.733
don't know how to access what the first,

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:58.360
the first words to the song are.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:02.458
But like you said, by the
time we got to Nashville,

00:23:02.458 --> 00:23:03.850
we had the songs figured out.

00:23:03.850 --> 00:23:06.430
I think the thing is is that I don't mind

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:11.060
and actually I look forward
to what's gonna happen

00:23:11.060 --> 00:23:13.370
in between the songs and what
stories are we gonna tell

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:15.946
and it's very loose and
where is it gonna go,

00:23:15.946 --> 00:23:20.580
what kinda bent are we gonna
come up with in the moment,

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:24.850
but I don't want all
the bits to come from--

00:23:24.850 --> 00:23:26.703
- Fails.
- Screwing the songs up.

00:23:26.703 --> 00:23:27.590
- Well, they didn't.
- And they didn't,

00:23:27.590 --> 00:23:32.550
they didn't, 'cause again,
I have a lot more fun,

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:34.540
the more I know the song--

00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:37.327
- It frees you up to then--
- Then I can actually have fun

00:23:37.327 --> 00:23:40.360
in the midst of it because it's like,

00:23:40.360 --> 00:23:43.820
it's just one of those, it's
a little bit of a blessing

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:46.321
and a curse that we get
to do so many things

00:23:46.321 --> 00:23:47.970
but because we do so many things,

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:50.509
it isn't like we sit
around and like rehearse--

00:23:50.509 --> 00:23:52.160
- Right.
- Our music all the time.

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:54.530
- We don't rehearse
enough but then we, again,

00:23:54.530 --> 00:23:58.840
we turn the mess ups into
okay, we can make fun

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:02.020
of that happening and we can
kinda milk it for comedy but--

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:03.760
- And maybe we'll--
- It's also a very safe space.

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:06.650
- Maybe we'll have, I mean
my goal is for us to have

00:24:06.650 --> 00:24:09.780
at least another new song
that no one has heard at all

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:10.737
by the time we--

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:14.750
- Yeah in June, in June.
- Go out on the summer tour.

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:17.260
- But it's also great, I'm very grateful

00:24:17.260 --> 00:24:20.020
that it's such a safe
space for us to perform

00:24:20.020 --> 00:24:22.440
because everybody is, even
if they didn't know what

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:24.787
to expect, they're a
dedicated Mythical Beast

00:24:24.787 --> 00:24:29.787
or they're there with someone
who's already oriented

00:24:30.630 --> 00:24:32.250
to what's going on so
it's not like we have

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:33.960
to win the crowd over.

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:36.620
We just have to give 'em,
we just have to have fun

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:41.620
and I think it was a lot
of fun and I can't imagine

00:24:41.830 --> 00:24:45.530
how if you're, if touring
is your first thing

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:47.120
and you're trying to win
over a crowd and trying

00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:50.550
to build an audience that
way, that would be difficult,

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:53.321
so it's nice that we don't--
- Oh yeah.

00:24:53.321 --> 00:24:54.300
- We don't have to do that.

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:57.210
- Well you know, speaking
of winning over a crowd

00:24:57.210 --> 00:24:58.990
who's not familiar with
you, one of the things

00:24:58.990 --> 00:25:03.733
that made this tour unique
was Britton opening up for us.

00:25:03.733 --> 00:25:05.130
- Yeah.

00:25:05.130 --> 00:25:07.330
- And of course, we talk
about Britton a lot,

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:10.420
Britton Buchanon, Link's
cousin who was on The Voice

00:25:10.420 --> 00:25:12.420
and is living with Link and Christy now.

00:25:13.466 --> 00:25:16.910
And we honestly didn't
know how that was gonna go

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:20.020
because his songs are
not, it's not comedy.

00:25:20.020 --> 00:25:20.853
- Right.
- He's just like a

00:25:20.853 --> 00:25:25.446
a legitimately great songwriter
and musician and singer

00:25:25.446 --> 00:25:28.700
and so we were like well, we enjoy this.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:30.470
We think you'll enjoy it too and you know

00:25:30.470 --> 00:25:35.470
that there's the family connection
but we would kinda go out

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:37.580
and watch, not go out into the crowd

00:25:37.580 --> 00:25:41.216
but kinda go side stage
and watch Britton perform

00:25:41.216 --> 00:25:44.220
and it's like thank you
to all the Mythical Beasts

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:46.700
who received him so warmly but I'll say

00:25:46.700 --> 00:25:49.731
that you weren't doing
it out of any sort of--

00:25:49.731 --> 00:25:51.610
- Obligation.
- Obligation or pity,

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:53.710
I think everyone was legitimate,

00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:55.992
everything that I've seen on the internet,

00:25:55.992 --> 00:25:59.921
everybody was legitimately
impressed because he's good.

00:25:59.921 --> 00:26:03.000
- Yeah, and he did a good
job winning people over

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:06.060
with the stuff that he would
say in between songs too so.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:08.707
- Yeah we won't spoil any surprises.

00:26:08.707 --> 00:26:11.508
I think we're gonna talk
a little bit more about

00:26:11.508 --> 00:26:14.950
what Britton does on tour on LTAT

00:26:14.950 --> 00:26:16.950
so we won't get into that too much here

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:19.770
but we also traveled with him.

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:21.650
So he was with us the entire time.

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:23.850
- Speaking of traveling, so we were,

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:27.042
we have the tour bus situation
and then on the tour bus,

00:26:27.042 --> 00:26:29.080
you got the front part
and then you go back

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:33.820
to the middle part and there's
these, I call 'em coffins.

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:36.460
You sleep in a coffin,
they're like two high,

00:26:36.460 --> 00:26:38.710
on some buses they're three high.

00:26:38.710 --> 00:26:39.710
- Well these were two high--
- And you're sleeping

00:26:39.710 --> 00:26:41.606
on this thing.

00:26:41.606 --> 00:26:43.810
- I mean you can imagine,
the first time we did this,

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:47.170
there were three high so--
- You can't sit up in it.

00:26:47.170 --> 00:26:50.113
- And I can't even, I
can't lay all the way down.

00:26:51.030 --> 00:26:54.778
They don't make them six foot seven.

00:26:54.778 --> 00:26:57.330
These things are probably--
- Six two,

00:26:57.330 --> 00:26:58.406
six three.

00:26:58.406 --> 00:26:59.500
- Uh--

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:01.510
- I could stretch out.
- I think they might--

00:27:01.510 --> 00:27:03.410
- At six foot--
- I think they might be

00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:06.070
just below my height, I
think it might be like six

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:10.780
and a half feet plus the siding
or whatever so six five so--

00:27:10.780 --> 00:27:12.180
- How do you sleep in there?

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:14.450
- Well not well.

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:17.070
- Because you couldn't--
- To begin with I'll say.

00:27:17.070 --> 00:27:19.286
For a couple reasons, I mean
not being able to stretch out,

00:27:19.286 --> 00:27:22.150
I sleep on my side so
I'm not at full height.

00:27:22.150 --> 00:27:26.127
I sleep like a baby in the womb and--

00:27:26.127 --> 00:27:27.600
- Like with your knees under your chin?

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:29.650
- I mean if I can get 'em there, yeah.

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:32.200
Completely balled up
ready for tornadoes and--

00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:34.060
- Like in a tuck position.

00:27:34.060 --> 00:27:37.068
- I sleep on my side so
I'm a little bit curled up

00:27:37.068 --> 00:27:41.190
but in my bed if I'm sleeping on my side,

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:44.070
there's moments during the
night where I'll kinda wake up

00:27:44.070 --> 00:27:45.730
and I'll just for a moment just sort

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:47.980
of stretch out completely being like--

00:27:47.980 --> 00:27:49.193
- Just unfurl the flag so to speak.

00:27:49.193 --> 00:27:51.640
- Just to remember my height.
- Length.

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:54.890
- You know and then turn to the other side

00:27:54.890 --> 00:27:58.070
so you can't do that and
also I'm such a light sleeper

00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:01.123
that pothole, I wake up.

00:28:02.300 --> 00:28:05.190
Fast turn, I wake up.

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:07.090
- Oh yeah?
- That's not fun.

00:28:07.090 --> 00:28:09.671
But I did get better.

00:28:09.671 --> 00:28:12.600
I did get better on the second night.

00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:15.070
- I like sleepin' in a confined space.

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:17.690
I don't know something
about it I just felt like

00:28:17.690 --> 00:28:19.260
a cozy little child.

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:21.330
- If it was a little bit
longer and we weren't moving--

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:22.380
- In a coffin.
- It would be great.

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:24.090
Once we arrived--
- That's a morbid thought.

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:28.020
- To the destination and
then they kinda let you

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:30.793
wake up on your own whenever
you happen to wake up,

00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:34.930
I'll sleep indefinitely in that scenario.

00:28:34.930 --> 00:28:36.850
- Well but you didn't.

00:28:36.850 --> 00:28:40.380
I mean that first morning there, I woke up

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:43.730
and I realized, I was starting
to realize we're not moving.

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:47.400
And your coffin has a curtain
so it's like pretty dark

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:48.920
in there even if it was bright outside

00:28:48.920 --> 00:28:52.460
which it turns out it was
'cause I looked out my curtain

00:28:53.570 --> 00:28:55.970
and then I got a text from
Jenna and she was like,

00:28:55.970 --> 00:28:59.040
instructions are on the
counter when you wanna come

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:02.940
into the hotel where, and I
assume where everybody else

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:05.680
already was because I didn't hear anything

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:08.699
and then so I got out,
no one was on the bus

00:29:08.699 --> 00:29:10.840
and then I look at
Britton's curtain's closed

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:12.550
and I don't open the curtain

00:29:12.550 --> 00:29:16.070
'cause I didn't know what
position or state he might be in

00:29:16.070 --> 00:29:20.080
in therms of clothing so
I just poked the curtain

00:29:20.971 --> 00:29:23.567
and I did hit something so
I'm like oh he is in there

00:29:23.567 --> 00:29:25.480
and I was like time to get up.

00:29:25.480 --> 00:29:26.888
It was 11:30.

00:29:26.888 --> 00:29:28.400
(chuckles)

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:31.140
Britton and I were the
only two people on the bus

00:29:31.140 --> 00:29:36.113
and we oriented ourselves
and we go to exit the bus

00:29:37.280 --> 00:29:40.983
and Britton goes down the steps
and he turns the doorknob.

00:29:41.950 --> 00:29:46.293
It doesn't respond and then
he's like shaking all the,

00:29:47.178 --> 00:29:49.547
he's really pulling on
the lever and I'm like,

00:29:49.547 --> 00:29:51.447
"Well hold on don't break the door."

00:29:52.370 --> 00:29:54.630
And he's like, "We're locked in, man."

00:29:54.630 --> 00:29:58.030
I'm like "Well no we're
not, just, you know,"

00:29:58.030 --> 00:30:00.160
and you look at the front of this bus

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:02.240
which we don't know anything
about and there's buttons,

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:04.750
it's like the cockpit of a airplane.

00:30:04.750 --> 00:30:06.040
There's buttons everywhere.

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:08.122
And so we start pushin' all of 'em.

00:30:08.122 --> 00:30:10.670
(chuckles) And we push
the ones that look like

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:12.870
they have something to do with a door.

00:30:12.870 --> 00:30:13.930
- Right.

00:30:13.930 --> 00:30:15.930
- And I heard something
click around the door

00:30:15.930 --> 00:30:18.759
but it still wouldn't open,
and then I'm over there

00:30:18.759 --> 00:30:20.830
puttin' all my weight onto it.

00:30:20.830 --> 00:30:23.092
We were locked on the bus.

00:30:23.092 --> 00:30:24.520
For a day.

00:30:24.520 --> 00:30:25.353
(Rhett chuckles)

00:30:25.353 --> 00:30:26.186
The whole day.

00:30:27.310 --> 00:30:28.540
No we eventually got off

00:30:28.540 --> 00:30:31.810
but that was our first bus experience.

00:30:31.810 --> 00:30:35.025
I like sleepin' on the bus but
I don't like being confined

00:30:35.025 --> 00:30:37.430
and not being able to exit the bus,

00:30:37.430 --> 00:30:38.620
that's a little disconcerting.

00:30:38.620 --> 00:30:40.830
- Well that raises an
interesting question.

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:42.990
Are there other exists?

00:30:42.990 --> 00:30:44.410
Is there an exit in the back?

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:47.450
Because there should be
exits because what if

00:30:47.450 --> 00:30:48.890
there was a fire--
- You thinkin' about this now?

00:30:48.890 --> 00:30:51.110
- What if there's a fire on the bus?

00:30:51.110 --> 00:30:52.340
There should be multiple exits

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:53.173
on the bus--
- I guess we need

00:30:53.173 --> 00:30:54.006
an orientation.

00:30:55.531 --> 00:31:00.031
- Obviously there's a exit
in the back of a school bus

00:31:00.031 --> 00:31:02.910
but it's a room in the back of this thing.

00:31:02.910 --> 00:31:05.020
- There's exits on the
roof of a school bus

00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:07.070
and every window is an exit.

00:31:07.070 --> 00:31:10.300
Like if a school bus lays
on its side or something,

00:31:10.300 --> 00:31:12.380
you can go out the top of a school bus.

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:14.905
- Right.
- Yeah or the window.

00:31:14.905 --> 00:31:16.700
- You can get out of a school
bus in lots of different ways.

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:18.880
- Every which way.
- You don't need a button.

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:19.713
- Need no button.

00:31:19.713 --> 00:31:21.240
- Maybe we should be
traveling in a school bus,

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:22.420
I'm just sayin'.

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:25.164
- Well you do have me
thinkin' that now I need

00:31:25.164 --> 00:31:26.913
to start checkin' for exits.

00:31:27.890 --> 00:31:29.610
- I'm a little worried.

00:31:29.610 --> 00:31:30.860
- That's why Christy
should have come with me.

00:31:30.860 --> 00:31:35.010
I know that's the first thing
she looks for is the exit

00:31:35.010 --> 00:31:35.913
out of everything.

00:31:38.620 --> 00:31:40.630
I mean not our relationship.

00:31:40.630 --> 00:31:42.080
We're doing great.

00:31:42.080 --> 00:31:44.030
- Hmm, interesting.

00:31:44.030 --> 00:31:45.920
- We played at Columbus
that night once I got out

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:48.720
of the bus and got off the scooter.

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:50.813
That was fun, that was a great show.

00:31:52.406 --> 00:31:55.080
I mean in general I'll just shout out,

00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:58.870
thanks to all the crowds
everywhere, super supportive

00:31:58.870 --> 00:32:01.653
and then when we do the
meet and greet afterward,

00:32:03.417 --> 00:32:06.908
it's always special because
you kinda get to hear

00:32:06.908 --> 00:32:10.183
just a little bit from fans in terms of

00:32:10.183 --> 00:32:15.183
how they watch the show, what
watching us means to them

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:19.470
and it always blows me away how

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:23.230
the nature of our show being daily

00:32:23.230 --> 00:32:25.770
and I guess our friendship
and I mean there's a lot

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:29.603
of intangibles that being on the inside,

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:32.847
it's just hard to get perspective

00:32:32.847 --> 00:32:37.060
on how people enjoy the show
or what it means to them

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:39.040
and helps them get through hard times

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:40.860
and they can count on
it and stuff like that

00:32:40.860 --> 00:32:45.300
so it's really helpful to get out there

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:46.670
and meet the people, you know?

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:48.470
To press the flesh.

00:32:48.470 --> 00:32:50.035
It really puts things in perspective.

00:32:50.035 --> 00:32:52.550
- I mean everything except that last part.

00:32:52.550 --> 00:32:54.563
I understand, I mean yes.

00:32:55.420 --> 00:32:57.620
- Press the flesh is not the right word.

00:32:57.620 --> 00:32:59.880
That was a political term
that you can't use anymore.

00:32:59.880 --> 00:33:02.203
- That was for handshakes and we do,

00:33:03.101 --> 00:33:04.639
if you want a hug, we hug.

00:33:04.639 --> 00:33:07.083
We let you initiate whatever you wanna do.

00:33:08.780 --> 00:33:10.270
But yeah I think that--

00:33:10.270 --> 00:33:13.550
- So flesh is pressed but I
mean it's upon their initiation.

00:33:13.550 --> 00:33:15.440
- Yeah I just don't like
to use flesh and press

00:33:15.440 --> 00:33:17.540
in the context of the
meet and greet, I just--

00:33:17.540 --> 00:33:18.540
- That's true.

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:20.170
- I'd just kinda like to
abandon that terminology.

00:33:20.170 --> 00:33:21.350
- Yeah let's abandon it.

00:33:21.350 --> 00:33:23.500
Let's lock it in the bus.

00:33:23.500 --> 00:33:26.030
- But I did see some
people on Twitter talking

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:28.563
before this run and they were saying,

00:33:30.817 --> 00:33:32.253
"I just, I don't know.

00:33:33.467 --> 00:33:36.287
"I don't know, I'm nervous.

00:33:36.287 --> 00:33:38.167
"I don't know what to say and,"

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:41.060
and I think people have
a tendency to feel like

00:33:41.060 --> 00:33:44.860
they have to have some
sort of something prepared

00:33:44.860 --> 00:33:48.010
or as if they're auditioning to be

00:33:48.010 --> 00:33:49.830
the perfect Mythical Beast in that moment

00:33:49.830 --> 00:33:52.981
and that's not what it's about at all.

00:33:52.981 --> 00:33:54.040
Whatever you wanna do,

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:56.900
I mean we have one girl who sang for us.

00:33:56.900 --> 00:33:59.230
- Got another guy who
performed a rap for us.

00:33:59.230 --> 00:34:00.780
- You know and those are awesome.

00:34:00.780 --> 00:34:02.117
- It's hilarious.
- If you wanna do that,

00:34:02.117 --> 00:34:03.090
do that.
- I mean it was supposed

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:04.080
to be funny and it was.

00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:06.670
- We don't encourage everyone to do that

00:34:06.670 --> 00:34:08.840
because I mean we'd be
there for seven hours

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:10.110
if everyone had a performance.

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:14.020
- We'd like for everyone
to perform some sort of,

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:15.410
bring your talent.

00:34:15.410 --> 00:34:17.420
- But ultimately what,
and some people are like,

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:20.840
you know what, I just, I
wanna hand you this note

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:23.495
because this is everything
that I want to say

00:34:23.495 --> 00:34:27.800
and it's more than I have
time to say in this time

00:34:27.800 --> 00:34:30.360
and some people are just like
hey I just want a picture.

00:34:30.360 --> 00:34:35.360
Whatever you want it to be,
just know that we're not,

00:34:35.380 --> 00:34:40.380
the way that we approach
this is we are 100% grateful.

00:34:42.990 --> 00:34:47.990
We are in a attitude of
gratitude, a stance of,

00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:49.960
mm--
- Prance.

00:34:49.960 --> 00:34:52.382
- Something that rhymes with sta,

00:34:52.382 --> 00:34:56.990
we're basically just
humbled that you guys come

00:34:56.990 --> 00:35:00.610
to the show, some of you
want to spend a little time

00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:01.892
with us and get a picture

00:35:01.892 --> 00:35:04.890
and we're just happy that you're there.

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:07.130
You don't have to say or do anything.

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:10.490
You're not fitting any
particular expectation so

00:35:10.490 --> 00:35:12.430
if that makes you feel
less nervous about it

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:15.460
then go with that because
we're not thinking about it

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:18.483
in that way, in fact, I heard,

00:35:19.500 --> 00:35:22.790
there was one girl at one of the shows

00:35:22.790 --> 00:35:27.560
who actually backed out
of the line before she got

00:35:27.560 --> 00:35:31.030
to the end and I think we're gonna end up

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:33.980
sending her something and
send her all the stuff

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:37.370
that she would have gotten in
the meet and greet line but--

00:35:37.370 --> 00:35:38.680
- It's kinda nerve-wracking--
- And I understand that.

00:35:38.680 --> 00:35:40.620
Some people deal with anxiety--

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:42.073
- It is nerve-wracking, man.
- More than others, I get it.

00:35:42.073 --> 00:35:44.610
- It's nerve-wracking to stand
in a line to meet somebody.

00:35:44.610 --> 00:35:46.050
- Oh I totally get that.

00:35:46.050 --> 00:35:49.670
- Because you can see the
line just kinda move along

00:35:49.670 --> 00:35:52.650
and then all of a sudden
you're like the next person.

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:57.650
I remember a few years back
when DanTDM came to Long Beach

00:35:59.870 --> 00:36:03.367
and Lincoln was a fan of his
and I was like I can get you

00:36:03.367 --> 00:36:06.283
in to see him, man, I got connections.

00:36:07.810 --> 00:36:10.658
And so we went in the meet and greet line

00:36:10.658 --> 00:36:15.658
and we're in the line and I
realized that I was nervous.

00:36:15.660 --> 00:36:17.400
I was like why am I nervous?

00:36:17.400 --> 00:36:20.500
Just by virtue of being in a
line with a bunch of people

00:36:20.500 --> 00:36:25.240
who are also nervous,
it's like contagious.

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:26.230
- Yeah.

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:28.820
- And you build this
expectation, it's like,

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:32.580
there is this moment of
okay, we're gonna shake hands

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:34.830
and I'm supposed to say something.

00:36:34.830 --> 00:36:36.773
I have to say something,
if I don't say anything,

00:36:36.773 --> 00:36:39.530
that could be weird, you know?

00:36:39.530 --> 00:36:41.640
- Yeah but even the people
who don't say anything,

00:36:41.640 --> 00:36:43.400
it's not weird for us.
- Everything like that

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:45.050
does make me anxious.

00:36:45.050 --> 00:36:49.090
- So I mean yeah and I'm not
saying at all don't be nervous,

00:36:49.090 --> 00:36:51.873
I'm just saying, just be
yourself, if yourself is nervous,

00:36:51.873 --> 00:36:54.850
then be nervous, if yourself is excited,

00:36:54.850 --> 00:36:59.770
then just be excited and there's
no judgment at all from us.

00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:03.450
And I know, listen, we're
bigger than you realize.

00:37:03.450 --> 00:37:05.113
- Physically.
- I am,

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:08.530
I know that I am intimidating physically.

00:37:08.530 --> 00:37:10.640
People are sometimes afraid to look at me

00:37:10.640 --> 00:37:13.360
because I think I'm
just like, I look like--

00:37:13.360 --> 00:37:14.240
- It's odd.

00:37:14.240 --> 00:37:16.780
- I look like Detlef Schrempf or whatever

00:37:16.780 --> 00:37:19.650
that NBA basketball player
was, you know what I'm saying?

00:37:19.650 --> 00:37:21.163
I'm a huge person.

00:37:22.170 --> 00:37:26.530
I have a lot of hair on my
face, I look like a hawk.

00:37:26.530 --> 00:37:28.710
- You're weird lookin', man.
- I've been called a falcon

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:31.230
by a drunk man in a van one time.

00:37:31.230 --> 00:37:32.993
- You look like a falcon.
- Man.

00:37:35.306 --> 00:37:39.070
- You look like a tall Detlef
Schrempf-lookin' falcon.

00:37:39.070 --> 00:37:42.503
- But I'm very soft and inviting.

00:37:44.121 --> 00:37:46.721
I have sweaty hands, you
can shake them if you want.

00:37:48.750 --> 00:37:50.443
- That didn't make it any less weird.

00:37:50.443 --> 00:37:53.000
(Rhett laughs)

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:55.430
We should talk about hot chicken.

00:37:55.430 --> 00:37:58.760
So we get to Nashville and
we had a little more time

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:00.970
in Nashville 'cause we had a one day break

00:38:03.358 --> 00:38:06.880
and man, by the time I got in line at,

00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:08.970
we decided to go to Hattie B's even though

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:11.660
Mythical Chef Josh told
us to get hot chicken

00:38:11.660 --> 00:38:14.623
from Bolton's Spicy Chicken and Fish.

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:18.950
And even though Wikipedia
says that Prince's

00:38:18.950 --> 00:38:22.330
is the originator of hot chicken.

00:38:22.330 --> 00:38:23.380
- We didn't go there.

00:38:24.485 --> 00:38:27.880
- We were kinda starving, delirious

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:30.230
and closest to Hattie B's.

00:38:30.230 --> 00:38:34.130
- And also we tend to be
very dependent upon Yelp.

00:38:34.130 --> 00:38:35.894
- And there's so many Yelp reviews.

00:38:35.894 --> 00:38:37.390
- A lot of people--
- Love it.

00:38:37.390 --> 00:38:39.910
- Don't like that, I mean
don't like when you're

00:38:39.910 --> 00:38:42.558
that dependent on Yelp
but I just, you know,

00:38:42.558 --> 00:38:45.390
4,000 reviews, four and a half stars,

00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:46.790
you know it's gonna be good.

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:50.670
- And we get there, we scootered there.

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:53.270
- Of course we did, nearly died.

00:38:53.270 --> 00:38:56.840
- We're there for a couple
of minutes and then we hear,

00:38:56.840 --> 00:38:59.120
well it's 45 minutes from
where you are in line.

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:01.790
- Yeah it was like
being on Space Mountain.

00:39:01.790 --> 00:39:04.720
- I showed up starving, I was like,

00:39:04.720 --> 00:39:08.335
I don't wait this long, I just don't.

00:39:08.335 --> 00:39:12.300
It doesn't add up, I started
looking for Bolton's.

00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:14.740
Like we could travel to
Bolton's via scooter,

00:39:14.740 --> 00:39:16.990
eat and be done and still come back

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:18.480
and not have gone through
this line probably.

00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:21.990
- Yeah but that to me, that's
all part of the experience

00:39:21.990 --> 00:39:24.450
of going to a place like
that and I will say,

00:39:24.450 --> 00:39:27.180
I do think it's worth
noting that hot chicken

00:39:27.180 --> 00:39:29.350
has been exported from Nashville

00:39:29.350 --> 00:39:31.770
and of course it's in
LA and there's a number

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:32.940
of places that serve it,

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:35.270
Howlin' Rays probably
being the most popular

00:39:35.270 --> 00:39:39.540
and I have seen the Howlin'
Rays line before but neither

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:41.480
of us and I don't know if
we were just holdin' out

00:39:41.480 --> 00:39:44.520
for Nashville or just
happened circumstantially--

00:39:44.520 --> 00:39:45.353
- And never been there.

00:39:45.353 --> 00:39:47.360
- We had never had hot chicken.

00:39:47.360 --> 00:39:49.740
- Well you know, we did our
Instagram stories from the line

00:39:49.740 --> 00:39:52.300
and then we finally get
up there and we order

00:39:52.300 --> 00:39:56.760
and it's, I, you know
what, I'll agree with you.

00:39:56.760 --> 00:39:59.290
I actually ended up
saying, I'll say it again,

00:39:59.290 --> 00:40:02.160
I'm glad that I waited 45 minutes

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:04.093
and I kinda learned my lesson.

00:40:06.110 --> 00:40:08.350
You talk about gettin' nervous in a line.

00:40:08.350 --> 00:40:11.010
I mean in this type of
line when the only thing

00:40:11.010 --> 00:40:13.780
you gotta do is pay and eat food, boy,

00:40:13.780 --> 00:40:15.572
that's a great scenario.

00:40:15.572 --> 00:40:20.240
It's a great payoff and you're
talking like Magic Mountain

00:40:20.240 --> 00:40:21.073
or Space Mountain--
- Are you saying

00:40:21.073 --> 00:40:23.000
we should serve hot chicken
at the meet and greet?

00:40:23.000 --> 00:40:24.409
- I'm saying in contrast--
- Logistically,

00:40:24.409 --> 00:40:26.560
that would be difficult--
- To that, you don't have

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:28.910
to say anything besides
order what you want

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:29.800
at the end and then--

00:40:29.800 --> 00:40:31.557
- You gotta pick your spice level though.

00:40:31.557 --> 00:40:36.557
- But my point is the wait
made it taste that much better.

00:40:37.220 --> 00:40:39.920
- Oh yeah, delayed gratification, man.

00:40:39.920 --> 00:40:43.670
It's the spice of life, it's
the hot chicken of life.

00:40:43.670 --> 00:40:47.920
- And so I don't like spicy
foods, I've been burned,

00:40:47.920 --> 00:40:51.220
pun intended, from the show
so now anything that's spicy

00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:54.990
to me is like, it's triggering physically

00:40:54.990 --> 00:40:57.670
so I start to feel a little nauseous,

00:40:57.670 --> 00:41:01.240
but I didn't wanna just
try mild or medium,

00:41:01.240 --> 00:41:02.830
I'm like, if it's called hot chicken

00:41:02.830 --> 00:41:04.910
I should order a hot.
- You gotta get hot.

00:41:04.910 --> 00:41:07.030
And for those of you who
aren't familiar with it,

00:41:07.030 --> 00:41:08.910
'cause I had an idea,

00:41:08.910 --> 00:41:12.122
I thought that hot
chicken was fried chicken

00:41:12.122 --> 00:41:13.400
with hot sauce on it.

00:41:13.400 --> 00:41:15.970
- Like basted on it,
kinda like a hot wing.

00:41:15.970 --> 00:41:17.913
- But it is not that.

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:24.020
It is chicken that has spice
actually in the breading,

00:41:24.429 --> 00:41:26.527
first of all, so that's the
first thing that happens

00:41:26.527 --> 00:41:29.020
and so if you were to peel back the skin,

00:41:29.020 --> 00:41:33.080
you would see a little red layer of spice

00:41:33.080 --> 00:41:36.830
that is part of, so it gets
all the way onto the chicken,

00:41:36.830 --> 00:41:38.140
and then on top of that,

00:41:38.140 --> 00:41:40.040
depending on what spice level you get,

00:41:40.990 --> 00:41:43.400
they add a dry rub.

00:41:43.400 --> 00:41:45.130
Now I'm sure there's some
places that do a sauce

00:41:45.130 --> 00:41:50.130
but these places do a dry rub on top of,

00:41:50.280 --> 00:41:53.650
so it's basically like a powdery thing.

00:41:53.650 --> 00:41:57.540
I think there might be some
sauce as well on Hattie B's.

00:41:57.540 --> 00:41:59.800
I couldn't really tell--
- It's a little,

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:01.460
there's a dryness and a wetness to it

00:42:01.460 --> 00:42:04.360
but there's definitely a redness to it

00:42:04.360 --> 00:42:07.600
that makes it a bit intimidating.

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:10.370
But I was so hungry.
- But the levels were

00:42:10.370 --> 00:42:13.380
southern, which means no spice and yes,

00:42:13.380 --> 00:42:16.460
someone in our group
who will remain nameless

00:42:16.460 --> 00:42:19.370
but he's one of Britton's
friends, that's what he got.

00:42:19.370 --> 00:42:20.470
- No one who works for us.

00:42:20.470 --> 00:42:23.163
- He's also a Carolina
fan so it all adds up.

00:42:25.380 --> 00:42:30.320
The next level is mild and
then medium and then hot

00:42:30.320 --> 00:42:33.193
and then damn hot and
then shut the cluck up.

00:42:34.360 --> 00:42:36.110
Those are the levels

00:42:36.110 --> 00:42:39.313
and we all went with hot.
- Hot.

00:42:40.220 --> 00:42:43.760
- So Jenna got hot, we
got hot, Britton got hot.

00:42:43.760 --> 00:42:45.840
- And let me tell you, it was hot.

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:47.590
But let me tell you something else.

00:42:48.550 --> 00:42:50.240
It tasted great.
- Flavor, man.

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:51.720
- Like the hotness tasted great.

00:42:51.720 --> 00:42:54.400
- So much flavor.
- And it was big and juicy,

00:42:54.400 --> 00:42:56.577
oh it was so good, I mean it was--

00:42:56.577 --> 00:42:59.410
- And you got the dark--
- I love a good

00:42:59.410 --> 00:43:02.130
food experience, man, you know.

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:05.000
And I think it's turned
me back onto hotness.

00:43:05.000 --> 00:43:08.310
When I'm ridin' back home comin' back,

00:43:08.310 --> 00:43:09.513
we landed in LA.

00:43:10.360 --> 00:43:13.910
It's an hour drive to
get back to the studio

00:43:13.910 --> 00:43:15.170
to get in our cars.

00:43:15.170 --> 00:43:16.890
I found myself on Yelp lookin'

00:43:16.890 --> 00:43:18.207
for the hot chicken places around here.

00:43:18.207 --> 00:43:20.150
- And there's quite a few.
- I'm like man,

00:43:20.150 --> 00:43:21.640
I'm back on the spicy train.

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:24.506
- We might have to have
a hot chicken taste test

00:43:24.506 --> 00:43:26.870
Instagram story like when we went around

00:43:26.870 --> 00:43:28.020
and tried to find the best taco,

00:43:28.020 --> 00:43:30.070
we might have to do that with hot chicken.

00:43:30.070 --> 00:43:34.900
- But I did not order the
upper echelons of hotness

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:37.820
like you did.
- So you can get a tender

00:43:37.820 --> 00:43:41.750
or a wing by itself for
$2 extra and add that

00:43:41.750 --> 00:43:43.270
to your order so a lot of people do this

00:43:43.270 --> 00:43:47.510
just as a novelty kinda thing
so I had a show, you know,

00:43:47.510 --> 00:43:50.310
and also I feel like I've eaten
the world's hottest pepper,

00:43:50.310 --> 00:43:54.920
twice, so I was like, you know what,

00:43:54.920 --> 00:43:57.600
I'm just gonna do a damn hot tender.

00:43:57.600 --> 00:44:00.180
And then Britton, you know, he's 19.

00:44:00.180 --> 00:44:02.760
His body can take anything at this point.

00:44:02.760 --> 00:44:04.980
He got a shut the cluck up wing.

00:44:04.980 --> 00:44:07.970
No, a tender, he got a
shut the cluck up tender.

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:12.970
And if you were following
along on our Instagram stories,

00:44:13.067 --> 00:44:15.630
@rhettmc and @linklamont--
- Oh thank you.

00:44:15.630 --> 00:44:17.370
- You would have seen all this happen.

00:44:17.370 --> 00:44:19.457
- And Britton's I guess,
you can shout his out.

00:44:19.457 --> 00:44:21.390
- @brittonbuchanon, follow
him on Instagram too

00:44:21.390 --> 00:44:23.860
because the action's happenin' over there

00:44:23.860 --> 00:44:25.548
when we're traveling.

00:44:25.548 --> 00:44:27.077
- It was funny 'cause (chuckles),

00:44:27.970 --> 00:44:29.950
he ate that thing, he thought
he was gonna be all right,

00:44:29.950 --> 00:44:32.660
and then his face just started crying.

00:44:32.660 --> 00:44:34.961
His eyeballs just sweated.
- Right.

00:44:34.961 --> 00:44:37.950
He cried quite a bit.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:44:37.950 --> 00:44:41.602
- The damn hot tender was
damn hot but it was like

00:44:41.602 --> 00:44:43.920
as I was eating it, I was like,

00:44:43.920 --> 00:44:45.837
I could have gotten the sandwich this way

00:44:45.837 --> 00:44:47.670
and I would have been okay.

00:44:47.670 --> 00:44:51.950
- He texted us and said
that his dad looked into it

00:44:51.950 --> 00:44:53.510
'cause he tried to talk
his dad into eating one.

00:44:53.510 --> 00:44:55.293
He said that it had--

00:44:55.293 --> 00:44:57.170
- The top shut the cluck up.

00:44:57.170 --> 00:45:00.380
- Yeah it had scorpion
pepper and ghost pepper

00:45:00.380 --> 00:45:01.730
in the--
- And habanero,

00:45:01.730 --> 00:45:04.013
equal parts of all three.
- Gosh.

00:45:04.013 --> 00:45:06.290
- And it's like a powder
which is incidentally,

00:45:06.290 --> 00:45:09.700
it's like the heat is super intense

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:11.810
but it dissipates more quickly

00:45:11.810 --> 00:45:14.740
because it doesn't have an
oil base to it the way that,

00:45:14.740 --> 00:45:18.590
it doesn't have an oil carrier
the way that sauce does,

00:45:18.590 --> 00:45:22.332
'cause sauce will get into
your skin because literally,

00:45:22.332 --> 00:45:25.030
when you get essential
oils and you put them

00:45:25.030 --> 00:45:28.410
into like a olive oil
carrier so they'll like--

00:45:28.410 --> 00:45:29.590
- Yeah.
- Be carried further

00:45:29.590 --> 00:45:30.920
and they go into your skin.

00:45:30.920 --> 00:45:32.400
That's why eating the pepper straight

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:34.570
because you've got that
oil and then even worse,

00:45:34.570 --> 00:45:36.200
when you eat a sauce.
- Mm-mm.

00:45:36.200 --> 00:45:38.820
- It sticks with you, you feel it later,

00:45:38.820 --> 00:45:40.640
you feel it in your stomach,
you feel it on the way out

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:43.820
and it's still incredibly
hot in each stage.

00:45:43.820 --> 00:45:45.993
Something about the dry rub makes this,

00:45:45.993 --> 00:45:49.880
it's like a flash, bang, of hotness

00:45:49.880 --> 00:45:52.740
but for me, an hour later I'd forgotten

00:45:52.740 --> 00:45:53.573
that I ate something so hot.

00:45:53.573 --> 00:45:54.850
- Well even when you're eating it,

00:45:54.850 --> 00:45:57.720
I highly recommend eating it
with a slaw which was great

00:45:57.720 --> 00:45:59.345
and a pickle.
- You need something creamy.

00:45:59.345 --> 00:46:00.973
- The pickle and the
bread that comes with--

00:46:00.973 --> 00:46:03.220
- You got every creamy side.
- Oh yeah.

00:46:03.220 --> 00:46:04.620
- You got the mac and cheese,

00:46:04.620 --> 00:46:05.860
the potato salad--
- Potato salad.

00:46:05.860 --> 00:46:07.700
- And the slaw.
- I did.

00:46:07.700 --> 00:46:10.540
- And I ended up getting collard
greens and beans of course,

00:46:10.540 --> 00:46:12.160
the beans are good.

00:46:12.160 --> 00:46:13.648
- That doesn't help with the heat though.

00:46:13.648 --> 00:46:15.310
- But they actually, the
collard greens had this

00:46:15.310 --> 00:46:17.230
enhancing effect 'cause of the vinegar,

00:46:17.230 --> 00:46:19.490
it enhanced the heat.
- That's tough.

00:46:19.490 --> 00:46:22.200
- So anyway Hattie B's was
excellent but of course,

00:46:22.200 --> 00:46:25.003
because we didn't wanna
come back and face Josh,

00:46:26.044 --> 00:46:30.504
and also Josh made an
incredible dinner recommendation

00:46:30.504 --> 00:46:35.140
for a place called Husk
that we went that was--

00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:37.210
- Gosh, that was amazing.
- One of the best meals

00:46:37.210 --> 00:46:39.620
we've ever had.
- Shout out to all you people

00:46:39.620 --> 00:46:41.300
working at Husk.

00:46:41.300 --> 00:46:43.450
The people in the
kitchen knew who we were.

00:46:44.300 --> 00:46:48.190
And started sending out
stuff that we didn't order.

00:46:48.190 --> 00:46:50.130
They stuffed us like pigs.
- Oh gosh.

00:46:50.130 --> 00:46:52.350
- They stuffed us with pigs.
- Yeah.

00:46:52.350 --> 00:46:53.420
- It was crazy.
- And every--

00:46:53.420 --> 00:46:54.830
- It was amazing!
- First of all,

00:46:54.830 --> 00:46:58.670
the waitress described the items

00:46:58.670 --> 00:47:03.050
and I feel like she spoke
for like 12 minutes unbroken

00:47:03.050 --> 00:47:04.403
describing the different items,

00:47:04.403 --> 00:47:07.540
then she would say things
like, well first we take this

00:47:07.540 --> 00:47:11.500
and we put it under ashes for 12 hours

00:47:11.500 --> 00:47:14.550
and then we take it and we
rub it across a pig's back

00:47:14.550 --> 00:47:16.930
until it squeals and then
we capture the squeal

00:47:16.930 --> 00:47:19.830
and we put it in a bottle
and we sprinkle it on top

00:47:19.830 --> 00:47:22.048
of the collards and then
we take the collards

00:47:22.048 --> 00:47:24.793
and we run them into the past.

00:47:25.906 --> 00:47:28.150
And then we bring them out of the past

00:47:28.150 --> 00:47:31.190
once they have the essence of the old days

00:47:31.190 --> 00:47:33.100
and then we bring them to the table

00:47:33.100 --> 00:47:34.820
and then we grind them
up and sprinkle them

00:47:34.820 --> 00:47:37.283
on the other foods that
you will be enjoying.

00:47:38.540 --> 00:47:41.716
I mean I had a frickin'
whiskey with ham in it.

00:47:41.716 --> 00:47:43.890
(Link chuckles)

00:47:43.890 --> 00:47:45.550
- Yeah that was crazy, man.

00:47:45.550 --> 00:47:47.390
- It was so good.

00:47:47.390 --> 00:47:48.400
- What a night.

00:47:48.400 --> 00:47:51.570
- And it's in an old house.

00:47:51.570 --> 00:47:54.280
It's in an old house that
apparently is haunted,

00:47:54.280 --> 00:47:56.640
so Husk, go to Husk.

00:47:56.640 --> 00:47:59.280
- It was a surgeon from
like the Civil War.

00:47:59.280 --> 00:48:01.700
- Civil War, yeah, but so
Josh told us to go to there

00:48:01.700 --> 00:48:03.780
so we felt like we had to
follow his recommendation.

00:48:03.780 --> 00:48:05.420
- But we also felt like
we couldn't come back

00:48:05.420 --> 00:48:08.658
until we went to Bolton's
Hot Chicken and Fish.

00:48:08.658 --> 00:48:09.491
- [Rhett] Right.

00:48:09.491 --> 00:48:11.663
- Hot, yeah, Spicy Chicken and Fish,

00:48:11.663 --> 00:48:12.856
that's what it's called.

00:48:12.856 --> 00:48:13.890
They got a cockatrice breathing fire,

00:48:13.890 --> 00:48:15.900
well they got a chicken
breathing fire out front.

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:18.950
- Again, Instagram, we showed you this.

00:48:18.950 --> 00:48:21.793
- They've got a sign when
you order there and it says,

00:48:22.890 --> 00:48:25.280
basically proceed at your own risk.

00:48:25.280 --> 00:48:28.913
Do not touch your eyes or babies.

00:48:31.430 --> 00:48:34.600
When eating our hot chicken and fish.

00:48:34.600 --> 00:48:36.940
And--
- Do not touch babies.

00:48:36.940 --> 00:48:39.020
- And someone had said--
- Think about that.

00:48:39.020 --> 00:48:42.830
- How bad would you feel
if you sauce a baby.

00:48:42.830 --> 00:48:44.890
Oh man I'm sorry, I just sauced your baby.

00:48:44.890 --> 00:48:46.270
- Well it's really dry rubbing a baby.

00:48:46.270 --> 00:48:48.591
- I'm so sorry, I didn't
mean to dry rub your baby.

00:48:48.591 --> 00:48:51.211
(Rhett chuckles)

00:48:51.211 --> 00:48:54.090
If you've got a nasty habit

00:48:54.090 --> 00:48:56.710
of dry rubbin' a baby in the eyes,

00:48:56.710 --> 00:49:00.110
do not go to Nashville.
- Yeah, right.

00:49:00.110 --> 00:49:02.100
- You're not even invited.
- Mm-mm.

00:49:02.100 --> 00:49:03.580
- You cannot, first of all,

00:49:03.580 --> 00:49:04.700
that's a habit you need to break.

00:49:04.700 --> 00:49:06.449
What are you doing touching babies' eyes?

00:49:06.449 --> 00:49:07.282
- Yeah.

00:49:07.282 --> 00:49:11.230
- If a baby's got an
eye booger, who cares?

00:49:11.230 --> 00:49:12.490
Just let him have it.

00:49:12.490 --> 00:49:14.380
It's actually probably a good thing,

00:49:14.380 --> 00:49:17.380
it's like a protective layer
at the corner of the eyes.

00:49:17.380 --> 00:49:18.980
- Yeah.
- You don't need to--

00:49:18.980 --> 00:49:20.130
- I think in general I think you shouldn't

00:49:20.130 --> 00:49:21.450
touch the face of a baby.

00:49:21.450 --> 00:49:23.890
- Don't touch babies' faces.

00:49:23.890 --> 00:49:26.190
- You know.
- Eyes, like oh look at,

00:49:26.190 --> 00:49:27.090
that baby's so cute.

00:49:27.090 --> 00:49:29.569
Can I rub its eyes, what?

00:49:29.569 --> 00:49:30.770
- Mm-hmm.

00:49:30.770 --> 00:49:32.180
- And to have to put up a sign,

00:49:32.180 --> 00:49:34.506
I mean what does that say
about the people of Nashville?

00:49:34.506 --> 00:49:35.844
A lot of baby eye touchers--

00:49:35.844 --> 00:49:36.750
- They been dry rubbin' babies.

00:49:36.750 --> 00:49:37.920
- Eye touchers.

00:49:37.920 --> 00:49:39.760
- They were dry rubbin'
babies left and right

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:41.297
before they put that sign up.

00:49:41.297 --> 00:49:42.463
- It's probably good for the baby though.

00:49:42.463 --> 00:49:45.030
- The spice levels at Bolton,
it's a different scale.

00:49:45.030 --> 00:49:46.180
- We were told this.

00:49:46.180 --> 00:49:50.130
- There's no spice and then there's light

00:49:50.130 --> 00:49:53.030
or something like that, then there's mild,

00:49:53.030 --> 00:49:55.310
then there's medium, then there's hot.

00:49:55.310 --> 00:49:57.150
- But they said whatever
you got at Hattie B's

00:49:57.150 --> 00:49:59.220
is what we were told by
two different people,

00:49:59.220 --> 00:50:01.089
go one level lower--
- Level down.

00:50:01.089 --> 00:50:03.690
- I'm grateful that we did.

00:50:03.690 --> 00:50:06.267
- So yeah and they were right, so--

00:50:06.267 --> 00:50:07.451
- It was a different experience.

00:50:07.451 --> 00:50:10.130
- Hot there was medium at Bolton's.

00:50:10.130 --> 00:50:11.980
And they don't have a sandwich there.

00:50:12.900 --> 00:50:15.663
It's just the fried
chicken, similar sides.

00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:19.293
I tried the beans, actually, well,

00:50:19.293 --> 00:50:22.483
I'll just go ahead and
say that not just Josh,

00:50:23.430 --> 00:50:27.310
but most Nashvillians that we ran into

00:50:27.310 --> 00:50:28.700
and said we had been to Hattie B's,

00:50:28.700 --> 00:50:30.290
they had this disappointed
look on their face,

00:50:30.290 --> 00:50:31.590
they were like yeah of course

00:50:31.590 --> 00:50:32.830
you guys did the touristy thing.

00:50:32.830 --> 00:50:35.213
You should really go to Bolton's,
you should go to Prince's.

00:50:36.050 --> 00:50:37.860
- Mm-hmm.
- Guys.

00:50:37.860 --> 00:50:39.720
- And we did, well we went to one.

00:50:39.720 --> 00:50:42.010
It's not--
- It's good.

00:50:42.010 --> 00:50:43.470
- Bolton's was really good.
- Really good.

00:50:43.470 --> 00:50:48.470
Really good and the
atmosphere was so authentic.

00:50:48.610 --> 00:50:52.423
- It's like cinder block
buildings that look like,

00:50:53.820 --> 00:50:55.750
they look like bunkers almost.

00:50:55.750 --> 00:50:57.850
Look like I was eatin'
at a barbecue bunker.

00:50:57.850 --> 00:51:01.530
- Right.
- Really, really rustic.

00:51:01.530 --> 00:51:03.163
- But we gotta say.

00:51:04.190 --> 00:51:07.210
- We both enjoyed Hattie B's
more, I mean it was just--

00:51:07.210 --> 00:51:08.350
- Yeah.
- It was more

00:51:08.350 --> 00:51:11.163
of a distinct thing.

00:51:12.070 --> 00:51:14.330
- Now they do have hot fish.

00:51:14.330 --> 00:51:19.240
You can get all the same
spices on fish at Bolton's

00:51:19.240 --> 00:51:22.510
and so we got a hot fish and
we went all the way to hot

00:51:23.485 --> 00:51:27.430
and they got whitefish, they
got catfish, they got grouper.

00:51:27.430 --> 00:51:29.050
We got the whitefish
'cause it was at the top

00:51:29.050 --> 00:51:31.620
of the menu, we figured that
was the one that was suggested.

00:51:31.620 --> 00:51:33.560
- I would recommend doing both though.

00:51:33.560 --> 00:51:35.370
- Yeah yeah you should try
that 'cause I never had

00:51:35.370 --> 00:51:37.934
a spicy fish like that, it was very good.

00:51:37.934 --> 00:51:39.700
- We should skip to the Ryman

00:51:41.030 --> 00:51:42.742
because we're at this
point in our conversation

00:51:42.742 --> 00:51:46.364
and we haven't gotten
to it but being a fan

00:51:46.364 --> 00:51:50.440
of old country music and we went through

00:51:50.440 --> 00:51:54.310
the Country Music Hall of
Fame and took our time,

00:51:54.310 --> 00:51:56.530
had a great time, they
had an outlaws exhibit

00:51:56.530 --> 00:51:59.280
which was amazing but
in a lot of the pictures

00:51:59.280 --> 00:52:01.360
that you'll see throughout the
Country Music Hall of Fame,

00:52:01.360 --> 00:52:05.530
you'll see people performing
at the Ryman Auditorium

00:52:05.530 --> 00:52:08.190
because it was the home
of the Grand Ole Opry

00:52:08.190 --> 00:52:09.513
for 30 years.

00:52:10.680 --> 00:52:13.720
- And lots of people--
- Which was the distinct,

00:52:13.720 --> 00:52:18.040
it was the place where country
music legends were made

00:52:18.040 --> 00:52:19.820
and certified.
- Yeah so basically anybody

00:52:19.820 --> 00:52:24.820
who's anybody in country
music like old country--

00:52:25.086 --> 00:52:26.510
- Yeah.
- Has played,

00:52:26.510 --> 00:52:27.700
and then of course--
- Hank Williams.

00:52:27.700 --> 00:52:29.510
- All the new acts have
played there as well because--

00:52:29.510 --> 00:52:30.343
- Elvis Presley.
- It's still got--

00:52:30.343 --> 00:52:33.700
- Played there.
- This incredible history.

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:36.096
- But the Ryman Auditorium
was originally built

00:52:36.096 --> 00:52:41.096
as an actual church like
a revival venue and--

00:52:41.817 --> 00:52:44.020
- And you can totally
tell when you go in there

00:52:44.020 --> 00:52:46.923
because it's all pews,
it's all a big circle.

00:52:46.923 --> 00:52:49.010
It's a huge balcony that follows

00:52:49.010 --> 00:52:51.670
the same semi-circular
pattern and then you've got

00:52:51.670 --> 00:52:56.080
the stained glass windows,
I mean super famous.

00:52:56.080 --> 00:52:58.560
It's super famous for the history

00:52:58.560 --> 00:53:00.470
but it's also super
famous for the acoustics

00:53:00.470 --> 00:53:03.880
because it's all wood.
- Yeah.

00:53:03.880 --> 00:53:07.450
- And the circular thing is just like one

00:53:07.450 --> 00:53:10.157
of the best sounding places
that you can play music.

00:53:10.157 --> 00:53:13.128
- And I'm doing some
research when we're in D.C.

00:53:13.128 --> 00:53:17.110
I wanna see Merle playing here and I mean

00:53:17.110 --> 00:53:19.770
he first played there as
he was starting his career

00:53:19.770 --> 00:53:23.940
in 1967 but then Johnny
Cash who was a member

00:53:23.940 --> 00:53:26.653
of the Grand Ole Opry
and was then kicked out

00:53:26.653 --> 00:53:29.820
and you can see that in
the movie Walk the Line,

00:53:29.820 --> 00:53:32.780
he was let go but then he came back

00:53:32.780 --> 00:53:35.890
when he had his own television
show The Johnny Cash show.

00:53:35.890 --> 00:53:39.670
He insisted that it be filmed
at the Ryman Auditorium,

00:53:39.670 --> 00:53:42.060
at the home of the Grand Ole Opry.

00:53:42.060 --> 00:53:44.980
So they would film his
show there and I mean,

00:53:44.980 --> 00:53:48.250
Johnny Cash is bringin'
Bob Dylan on his show.

00:53:48.250 --> 00:53:53.250
I mean this is an amazing
institution within an institution,

00:53:53.480 --> 00:53:58.480
just his show, and he had as
Merle was gaining popularity,

00:54:00.474 --> 00:54:05.310
he comes on the show and
seeing the two of them interact

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:08.150
is like, it just gives
me goosebumps thinking

00:54:08.150 --> 00:54:13.150
about that footage but one
story in particular is amazing,

00:54:13.350 --> 00:54:17.997
that of course, Merle was in prison.

00:54:20.840 --> 00:54:23.510
And just like the lyric in Mama Tried,

00:54:23.510 --> 00:54:25.930
he did turn 21 in prison.

00:54:25.930 --> 00:54:28.310
He wasn't serving life
without parole but--

00:54:28.310 --> 00:54:31.360
- 15 years of something like that.

00:54:31.360 --> 00:54:35.940
- And Johnny Cash knew this
but he encouraged Merle

00:54:35.940 --> 00:54:39.100
to talk about it openly,
his time in prison,

00:54:39.100 --> 00:54:40.970
which he hadn't done, now some people knew

00:54:40.970 --> 00:54:42.810
that he had been in prison but it was

00:54:42.810 --> 00:54:46.810
on The Johnny Cash Show, on
the stage that we performed on

00:54:46.810 --> 00:54:49.495
that Merle and a
conversation with Johnny Cash

00:54:49.495 --> 00:54:54.495
told the world for the
first time that he was...

00:54:55.798 --> 00:54:59.560
He was a prisoner, he was an ex-con.

00:54:59.560 --> 00:55:01.320
- Mm-hmm.
- And not only that,

00:55:01.320 --> 00:55:03.790
he told it in the context of the story of

00:55:03.790 --> 00:55:07.570
when Johnny Cash on
many occasions would go

00:55:07.570 --> 00:55:10.180
and perform concerts at prisons,

00:55:10.180 --> 00:55:13.210
Folsom Prison concert is a famous album

00:55:13.210 --> 00:55:17.220
but so is his San Quentin performance.

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:19.830
But he performed at San
Quentin a number of times

00:55:19.830 --> 00:55:23.880
and one of those times, Merle
Haggard was in the audience.

00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:25.010
- [Rhett] Mm-hmm.

00:55:25.010 --> 00:55:28.740
- As a frickin' inmate and
he's like telling that story

00:55:28.740 --> 00:55:32.440
so it's basically his coming
out that hey I was an ex-con

00:55:32.440 --> 00:55:36.470
and blowing people's minds that Johnny,

00:55:36.470 --> 00:55:39.573
I was at your concert as an inmate and so,

00:55:41.172 --> 00:55:42.230
and to know that happened on that stage

00:55:42.230 --> 00:55:45.280
just kinda blew my mind
and then I realized, man,

00:55:45.280 --> 00:55:46.410
I'm gettin' nervous.

00:55:46.410 --> 00:55:49.073
- Well I was already nervous.

00:55:49.073 --> 00:55:51.060
(chuckles)
Before we went to

00:55:51.060 --> 00:55:55.598
the hall of fame, because first of all,

00:55:55.598 --> 00:55:57.540
everyone who we talked to,

00:55:57.540 --> 00:55:58.600
if we say we're playing the Ryman,

00:55:58.600 --> 00:56:00.520
like you're playing the Ry--
- Yeah.

00:56:00.520 --> 00:56:03.655
- You're going to this sacred place

00:56:03.655 --> 00:56:07.350
and I'm also thinking
about the thing that it is

00:56:07.350 --> 00:56:11.610
that we do which is, we're
not country musicians,

00:56:11.610 --> 00:56:16.090
I mean, we're gonna be actin'
like fools up in this place,

00:56:16.090 --> 00:56:18.960
you know, we're playing
music but it's stupid

00:56:18.960 --> 00:56:20.040
in a lot of ways.

00:56:20.040 --> 00:56:22.010
- We got a lot of testicle jokes.

00:56:22.010 --> 00:56:23.840
- Yeah, and so--
- And we've got a whole

00:56:23.840 --> 00:56:26.410
rap set which feels weird.
- Which feels a little

00:56:26.410 --> 00:56:29.930
out of place in the Ryman but
so in seeing all that footage

00:56:29.930 --> 00:56:32.490
and people who made their debut there

00:56:32.490 --> 00:56:34.830
because one of the cool things about

00:56:34.830 --> 00:56:36.440
the Country Music Hall of Fame in addition

00:56:36.440 --> 00:56:41.083
to the memorabilia, the
archival sorta stuff,

00:56:42.922 --> 00:56:47.021
or the artifacts is all the
video that they have in there.

00:56:47.021 --> 00:56:48.470
- Right.
- They've got so,

00:56:48.470 --> 00:56:51.734
all these, like you said the
footage of the conversation

00:56:51.734 --> 00:56:53.820
on The Johnny Cash Show with Merle,

00:56:53.820 --> 00:56:56.310
but so much of that and
so many pictures I'm like,

00:56:56.310 --> 00:56:57.950
that's the Ryman, that's
the Ryman, that's the Ryman,

00:56:57.950 --> 00:57:00.950
and then I kept saying like
we're playing there tonight

00:57:00.950 --> 00:57:04.310
and I started thinking a couple things

00:57:04.310 --> 00:57:08.580
like A, I'm way more
nervous than I usually am

00:57:08.580 --> 00:57:09.413
before a show.

00:57:10.639 --> 00:57:15.639
And two, we had decided
to play two Merle songs.

00:57:15.850 --> 00:57:17.740
Two of our favorite Merle songs.

00:57:17.740 --> 00:57:18.760
- Which is something we've never done.

00:57:18.760 --> 00:57:20.920
We don't cover anybody's songs

00:57:20.920 --> 00:57:24.300
and we've never performed
Merle's songs publicly

00:57:24.300 --> 00:57:25.460
that I can remember.

00:57:25.460 --> 00:57:30.350
- Right and so we did one
that is a really popular

00:57:30.350 --> 00:57:32.510
old school Merle song and
then one that's a little bit

00:57:32.510 --> 00:57:37.460
of a deeper cut and I was thinking

00:57:39.350 --> 00:57:41.691
I might cry.
- Oh.

00:57:41.691 --> 00:57:43.503
- I was like I don't want, you know,

00:57:44.380 --> 00:57:46.520
I don't wanna cry during this thing.

00:57:46.520 --> 00:57:49.510
Because I can't cry and
still continue to sing.

00:57:49.510 --> 00:57:52.170
I'm not one of those people
who can like, you know--

00:57:52.170 --> 00:57:53.460
- Cry-sing?
- Cry-sing.

00:57:53.460 --> 00:57:54.293
Some people can.

00:57:54.293 --> 00:57:55.856
- Kinda like Vince Gill.

00:57:55.856 --> 00:57:58.131
He seems like he's always cry-singing.

00:57:58.131 --> 00:58:00.980
- My voice would break and so I was like

00:58:00.980 --> 00:58:03.650
I don't want that to happen so you know,

00:58:03.650 --> 00:58:06.270
I don't know how I'm gonna
take my mind off that.

00:58:06.270 --> 00:58:11.270
But a few things about the
show, I mean first of all,

00:58:11.300 --> 00:58:13.560
thankfully we did have everything down

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:15.930
from a musical standpoint
by the time we got there.

00:58:15.930 --> 00:58:19.040
It did sound like we could
hear ourselves really well

00:58:19.040 --> 00:58:23.623
and you sorta feel the power of the music

00:58:25.670 --> 00:58:27.550
kinda going out and
coming around that circle

00:58:27.550 --> 00:58:29.320
and coming back to you.

00:58:29.320 --> 00:58:33.120
And also, you can hear every single thing

00:58:33.120 --> 00:58:37.230
that is being, the crowd
reaction is so immediate

00:58:37.230 --> 00:58:39.830
'cause they're right there
on you and it's 2200 people,

00:58:39.830 --> 00:58:43.230
it's a small, small venue
and they're kinda on top

00:58:43.230 --> 00:58:45.930
of you in a lot of ways 'cause
you got that huge balcony,

00:58:45.930 --> 00:58:48.620
so the furthest person from
you is still relatively close.

00:58:48.620 --> 00:58:50.090
- And it makes such a big difference.

00:58:50.090 --> 00:58:54.000
I mean in the little bit
of touring that we've done,

00:58:54.000 --> 00:58:56.760
it's like, every venue is different

00:58:56.760 --> 00:58:58.277
and you never know exactly
what you're gonna get

00:58:58.277 --> 00:59:01.090
and what crowd is gonna show up.

00:59:01.090 --> 00:59:02.560
But everything lined up, I mean,

00:59:02.560 --> 00:59:05.956
the crowd was so
boisterous and responsive.

00:59:05.956 --> 00:59:06.876
- Oh yeah.

00:59:06.876 --> 00:59:08.490
- You know I had somebody,
I don't know how we got

00:59:08.490 --> 00:59:12.230
to this me asking for anti-bacterial soap

00:59:12.230 --> 00:59:13.490
and then somebody's like throwing--

00:59:13.490 --> 00:59:15.480
- Hand sanitizer.
- Hand sanitizer up

00:59:15.480 --> 00:59:20.410
at me and like, I
emptied the entire bottle

00:59:20.410 --> 00:59:23.230
that she threw to me into my hand

00:59:23.230 --> 00:59:24.750
and then I didn't know what to do with it

00:59:24.750 --> 00:59:26.250
and we came up with something.

00:59:27.225 --> 00:59:28.800
It was a lot of fun, man,
everybody was just lovin',

00:59:28.800 --> 00:59:30.640
everybody was having a good time.

00:59:30.640 --> 00:59:32.240
So by the time in the middle of the set

00:59:32.240 --> 00:59:35.790
when we performed Silver Wings,

00:59:35.790 --> 00:59:37.740
you felt like you weren't gonna cry?

00:59:37.740 --> 00:59:41.506
'Cause people, like, people
said that they cried.

00:59:41.506 --> 00:59:44.580
And I was like yes.
- It was very, very emotional

00:59:44.580 --> 00:59:47.682
for me, like playing both
that and then Driftwood,

00:59:47.682 --> 00:59:49.870
and we played Driftwood with Britton,

00:59:49.870 --> 00:59:53.120
he got up there and killed
the solo, the guitar solo

00:59:53.120 --> 00:59:56.620
and it was emotional for sure

00:59:56.620 --> 00:59:59.270
but I didn't think that
I was gonna break down

00:59:59.270 --> 01:00:00.990
at that moment.

01:00:00.990 --> 01:00:04.410
So the show just went on, it was great,

01:00:04.410 --> 01:00:07.670
and we got to that last song that we sing

01:00:07.670 --> 01:00:09.730
which is a little bit of
an emotional, it's funny,

01:00:09.730 --> 01:00:12.710
but it's got an emotional
quality and tone to it.

01:00:12.710 --> 01:00:14.783
The song that we close the show out with,

01:00:15.990 --> 01:00:18.470
and you knew this.

01:00:18.470 --> 01:00:20.650
Most people probably
didn't pick up on the fact

01:00:20.650 --> 01:00:22.430
that I flubbed two lyrics.

01:00:22.430 --> 01:00:25.000
We ended up just changing
in the moment one lyric

01:00:25.000 --> 01:00:26.330
that was from a different part of the song

01:00:26.330 --> 01:00:28.340
and there was one part
where I didn't come in

01:00:28.340 --> 01:00:30.660
but you sang that part,
whatever, which by this point

01:00:30.660 --> 01:00:32.620
in the tour wasn't typical.
- Right.

01:00:32.620 --> 01:00:34.600
- Especially that song.

01:00:34.600 --> 01:00:35.433
And I told you later,

01:00:35.433 --> 01:00:37.410
I was like--
- What was happening?

01:00:37.410 --> 01:00:41.218
- I was so in the moment

01:00:41.218 --> 01:00:46.218
and thinking that I cannot
believe how much fun this is.

01:00:47.810 --> 01:00:51.340
I cannot believe how good this is.

01:00:51.340 --> 01:00:52.650
You know what I'm sayin', not good like,

01:00:52.650 --> 01:00:54.270
I can't believe how good we are,

01:00:54.270 --> 01:00:57.540
just how good this is all lining up.

01:00:57.540 --> 01:01:00.570
- Yeah you were savoring it,
man, I think that's great.

01:01:00.570 --> 01:01:02.503
Yeah.
- I lost the ability

01:01:02.503 --> 01:01:07.503
to be in the song 'cause
I was so in the moment.

01:01:07.640 --> 01:01:10.375
It's like I kinda left
my body for a second

01:01:10.375 --> 01:01:13.900
and took a little flight
around the Ryman and communed

01:01:13.900 --> 01:01:16.910
with the spirits of old country.

01:01:16.910 --> 01:01:19.250
And then came back in
in my body and was like,

01:01:19.250 --> 01:01:20.708
oh, I should be singing.

01:01:20.708 --> 01:01:21.830
(chuckles)

01:01:21.830 --> 01:01:24.853
- Yeah, it was magical, man, I mean,

01:01:25.760 --> 01:01:28.823
was it the peak of our career?

01:01:30.640 --> 01:01:32.710
Yeah, I mean--

01:01:32.710 --> 01:01:35.140
- It hasn't gotten any better
than that for me personally.

01:01:35.140 --> 01:01:37.070
- Well it was two days ago.

01:01:37.070 --> 01:01:42.070
- No I'm saying, people are
like, what's the best moment

01:01:42.930 --> 01:01:44.620
in your career--
- Oh wow.

01:01:44.620 --> 01:01:49.620
- I cannot think of any time
where I felt as fulfilled

01:01:53.244 --> 01:01:55.240
in what we get to do.
- Right.

01:01:55.240 --> 01:01:57.800
- As in that moment.
- Because the other things,

01:01:57.800 --> 01:02:00.300
I mean you put out the internet
video and it's just like

01:02:00.300 --> 01:02:02.480
you can only be so
fulfilled by the response

01:02:02.480 --> 01:02:06.960
of the comments, I mean, if you make,

01:02:06.960 --> 01:02:10.440
if you craft a documentary or a series

01:02:10.440 --> 01:02:13.660
or anything and you
screen it and then people,

01:02:13.660 --> 01:02:15.820
you're watching people
from the back of the room

01:02:15.820 --> 01:02:18.753
and then they're clapping, it's
like that is very thrilling.

01:02:19.809 --> 01:02:22.880
But it's not the same as performing.

01:02:22.880 --> 01:02:24.470
I mean, as having that moment

01:02:24.470 --> 01:02:28.939
of all the faces are staring
back at you and you're like,

01:02:28.939 --> 01:02:32.510
I'm savoring this like the first bite

01:02:32.510 --> 01:02:34.210
of hot chicken I ever had.

01:02:34.210 --> 01:02:39.210
You never have, I mean, you
could read about hot chicken,

01:02:39.410 --> 01:02:41.650
you can make analogies about hot chicken

01:02:41.650 --> 01:02:43.743
at the end of podcasts.
- Right.

01:02:44.730 --> 01:02:48.720
- But until you eat the
hot chicken on the stage

01:02:48.720 --> 01:02:50.610
of the Ryman.
- Mm-hmm.

01:02:50.610 --> 01:02:52.940
- Oh shoot, that would have been peak.

01:02:52.940 --> 01:02:54.581
That's, we blew it.

01:02:54.581 --> 01:02:55.834
- That would have been too much.

01:02:55.834 --> 01:02:57.810
That would have been sensory overload.

01:02:57.810 --> 01:02:59.180
- That's gonna be our next tour.

01:02:59.180 --> 01:03:00.631
I mean that's what
people want anyway right?

01:03:00.631 --> 01:03:02.060
- The hot chicken tour?
- Yeah we're just gonna get

01:03:02.060 --> 01:03:03.779
on stage and eat hot chicken.

01:03:03.779 --> 01:03:05.570
- (sighs) Well and that's another thing,

01:03:05.570 --> 01:03:06.880
I'm glad you brought that up.

01:03:06.880 --> 01:03:08.850
Not the hot chicken but the eating thing

01:03:08.850 --> 01:03:11.430
because you know me and we've
talked about this before,

01:03:11.430 --> 01:03:14.377
it's like, another reason
that it's so fulfilling to me,

01:03:14.377 --> 01:03:17.650
it's like okay it's cool
when we, it's like oh,

01:03:17.650 --> 01:03:20.680
we got to, oh there's 15
million people subscribed

01:03:20.680 --> 01:03:23.283
to Good Mythical Morning
and that's awesome.

01:03:24.360 --> 01:03:27.780
And it's, you know what,
it's why people show up

01:03:27.780 --> 01:03:29.870
at our frickin' show, let's be honest.

01:03:29.870 --> 01:03:31.740
It's why people can show
up and watch us sing.

01:03:31.740 --> 01:03:35.900
They're not showing up to
watch us sing primarily,

01:03:35.900 --> 01:03:37.680
most people because they've seen us sing,

01:03:37.680 --> 01:03:39.440
they've seen us do weird
stuff on the internet

01:03:39.440 --> 01:03:41.263
and they wanna come hang out with us.

01:03:41.263 --> 01:03:46.263
But because I truly believe
that we crafted something.

01:03:47.514 --> 01:03:52.514
We have crafted a show that I
feel good about artistically

01:03:53.020 --> 01:03:54.780
and creatively and again
that's the kinda thing

01:03:54.780 --> 01:03:56.920
that kinda gets me going, right?

01:03:56.920 --> 01:03:58.810
Obviously I love the
stuff that's not planned

01:03:58.810 --> 01:04:00.140
and the humor that happens in the moment

01:04:00.140 --> 01:04:02.940
and the things that make
that particular show unique,

01:04:02.940 --> 01:04:05.951
but the thing that really
gets me going is like

01:04:05.951 --> 01:04:10.270
sitting down and over the
course of a labored process,

01:04:10.270 --> 01:04:15.270
creating something special
that then is crafted

01:04:15.494 --> 01:04:19.860
with the real time reception
of an audience in mind.

01:04:19.860 --> 01:04:21.120
You know what I'm saying?
- Yeah.

01:04:21.120 --> 01:04:23.775
And it's very rewarding
when it comes together.

01:04:23.775 --> 01:04:24.608
- And then being able to go out there

01:04:24.608 --> 01:04:28.056
and actually execute it
and see the real time,

01:04:28.056 --> 01:04:31.940
feel the real time
reaction from an audience

01:04:31.940 --> 01:04:33.993
in a place that's so special,

01:04:35.001 --> 01:04:37.457
the combination of those things

01:04:37.457 --> 01:04:39.940
and the way that it came
together, I was just like,

01:04:39.940 --> 01:04:42.354
it doesn't get any better than
this, I mean like we could,

01:04:42.354 --> 01:04:45.740
hopefully we'll continue
to do great things.

01:04:45.740 --> 01:04:49.180
Hopefully we've got bigger
and better things ahead of us

01:04:50.643 --> 01:04:53.483
and there's other creative
things that we want to do.

01:04:54.410 --> 01:04:57.520
But regardless of how well those things go

01:04:57.520 --> 01:05:01.230
and how well they're
received, it can only match,

01:05:01.230 --> 01:05:04.610
in my mind it can only match
the way I felt at the Ryman.

01:05:04.610 --> 01:05:07.920
- I agree with that, yeah,
yeah, I feel that way too.

01:05:07.920 --> 01:05:11.430
I'm just, and it, again going
out there and doing a show,

01:05:11.430 --> 01:05:13.460
it puts everything else
we've done in perspective

01:05:13.460 --> 01:05:14.960
because we wouldn't be
able to do it if it weren't

01:05:14.960 --> 01:05:19.890
for the other things but I'm
grateful for the experience

01:05:19.890 --> 01:05:23.130
because of everything you
said and also because it...

01:05:25.770 --> 01:05:27.810
It gives us perspective that we can't have

01:05:27.810 --> 01:05:30.680
when we just sit behind our desk.

01:05:30.680 --> 01:05:31.513
- Yeah.

01:05:31.513 --> 01:05:35.283
And like we say, we say
at the shows, it's like,

01:05:37.535 --> 01:05:41.420
there's all kinds of
amazing things that have,

01:05:41.420 --> 01:05:44.780
all the interactions that
we have with people online

01:05:44.780 --> 01:05:49.780
are invaluable and amazing
and in volume, much greater.

01:05:52.220 --> 01:05:53.850
There's just way more of
'em because it's the nature

01:05:53.850 --> 01:05:57.830
of the internet but there
is just something different

01:05:57.830 --> 01:06:02.040
about being in a room
full of Mythical Beasts.

01:06:02.040 --> 01:06:04.734
It's like we just don't
get to experience that.

01:06:04.734 --> 01:06:07.727
We make this podcast, we do the show,

01:06:07.727 --> 01:06:10.133
we do these other things
and people experience it

01:06:10.133 --> 01:06:13.532
on this regular basis and
have these special connections

01:06:13.532 --> 01:06:16.653
and then we hear about
them retrospectively.

01:06:17.620 --> 01:06:20.770
And that's really special
but to be in the same room

01:06:20.770 --> 01:06:24.295
while it's all happening
and the immediate feedback,

01:06:24.295 --> 01:06:28.720
and I feel like we've
got a very, first of all,

01:06:28.720 --> 01:06:31.076
our audience is very
eclectic in a lot of ways

01:06:31.076 --> 01:06:33.970
just in terms of where people are from,

01:06:33.970 --> 01:06:35.683
their background, their ages.

01:06:38.560 --> 01:06:42.360
There's also something very uniform

01:06:42.360 --> 01:06:45.370
about the way people are just Mythical,

01:06:45.370 --> 01:06:46.950
you know what I'm saying, so there's a lot

01:06:46.950 --> 01:06:49.070
of like-minded people in the same room

01:06:49.070 --> 01:06:51.202
who are just like connecting,

01:06:51.202 --> 01:06:54.080
not just with us but with each other.

01:06:54.080 --> 01:06:58.240
Being in that atmosphere is just special.

01:06:58.240 --> 01:07:01.512
- Yeah so again I wanna thank you for,

01:07:01.512 --> 01:07:04.050
if you've come out to one
of our shows, thank you,

01:07:04.050 --> 01:07:06.410
